const INDEX = [{"t": "Build 2 · Team essay", "a": "Team Charlotte", "u": "build-2.html", "x": "At first, Paradise Lost and Westworld seem to approach consciousness from opposite directions. Milton writes about human beings, temptation, and the Fall, while Westworld follows artificial beings struggling to understand minds built and programmed by others. Yet both works repeatedly unsettle the distinction between thinking and choosing. A person or host may reason, question, remember, and recognize alternatives without necessarily controlling the beliefs and desires that make one alternative seem preferable to another. Reading Milton alongside Westworld therefore opens questions that neither work answers easily: Does consciousness give us control over our actions, or merely awareness of them? Can a thought count as our own if we never chose the experiences that produced it? And if outside forces shape the mind before deliberation even begins, what would genuine agency require? Our individual readings approach these problems from different moments in Paradise Lost, revealing how Milton’s characters illuminate the same uncertainties about consciousness that Westworld places at the center of its hosts’ experience. Consider first Satan’s struggle to claim control over his own mind. In Build 1, we analyzed Satan’s soliloquy, where he confronts the consequences of his decision and wishes to repent, yet his impulses prevent his return. His struggle reveals the misery produced by his lack of conscious autonomy, while the soliloquy traces his slow movement from recognizing that lack of control to reclaiming a sense of control through after-the-fact rationalization. The hosts of Westworld follow a similar progression: they move from scripted responses toward seemingly autonomous choices, only to discover that even their experience of autonomy may follow a script. Epiphenomenalism therefore pushes Westworld beyond the question of whether AI can develop consciousness toward a more unsettling possibility: consciousness itself may follow a script, regardless of whether its subject enters the world born or built. But recognizing a script does not settle the harder question of what someone can do with the desires that script produces. Can we call a desire our own if someone else planted it? Eve imagines using knowledge to surpass Adam, asking, “for inferior who is free?” (825). Her reasoning challenges her assigned position while retaining the hierarchy that makes superiority seem necessary for freedom. Yet this dependence need not render her choice meaningless: Eve redirects the desire for advancement toward knowledge that obedience forbids. Westworld’s first-season finale tests a similar possibility through Maeve. Bernard’s revelation that her rebellion was programmed undermines her assumption that escaping the park necessarily establishes independence, just as Eve’s reasoning unsettles the equation of disobedience with freedom. Maeve nevertheless abandons the train to return for her daughter, turning a maternal attachment supplied by the park into a reason to interrupt her programmed escape. Read together, these decisions suggest that agency depends less on originating desire than on determining what authority it will have. Eve makes knowledge a reason to reject obedience; Maeve makes attachment a reason to reject escape. Maeve’s inherited love thus clarifies how Eve’s inherited ambition might sustain a chosen commitment, while Eve’s continuing investment in hierarchy cautions against treating either decision as complete liberation from the structures that shaped it. Determining what authority a desire should have becomes more difficult when a mind cannot identify where its thoughts originate. Eve tries to persuade Adam to eat the fruit, and Milton writes that “excuse / Came prologue, and apology to prompt” (854), suggesting that her justification forms before she consciously presents it to him. Yet Milton also says that Eve speaks “at will” (855), so she experiences the reasoning as her own. Jaynes complicates that experience with his claim that “we cannot be conscious of what we are not conscious of.” If we never witness a thought arrive, then we cannot easily determine what produced it. Westworld develops the same problem through Dolores, who believes that Arnold’s voice directs her thoughts until the finale forces her to recognize that the voice comes from within her own mind. For Jaynes, that recognition marks a crucial step toward consciousness because Dolores begins to identify the source of the voice rather than simply obeying it. Eve never reaches the same moment: she trusts the reasoning that enters her mind without examining where it came from, leaving consciousness dependent not only on thinking, but on recognizing the processes that shape thought itself. Recognizing where a thought comes from, however, does not establish whether conscious thought causes the action that follows. Eve reasons repeatedly about the fruit before Milton abruptly describes the decisive moment: “she plucked, she [ate]” (781). The simplicity of these verbs sharply contrasts with the complexity of Eve’s preceding thoughts, condensing the transition from consciousness to physical action into two immediate movements. Milton further describes her hand as “rash,” a word that complicates any straightforward claim that her action simply follows from the reasoning in her head. Westworld raises the same problem through Dolores, whose growing awareness appears to grant her greater control as she eventually acts against Ford. Yet the series simultaneously reveals how deeply programming shapes the actions she experiences as her own. Eve and Dolores therefore expose the same uncertainty: consciousness may accompany, interpret, or even reshape action without necessarily causing it, leaving open the possibility that awareness and control do not always arrive together. The limits on consciousness also appear at the level of perception itself. What does it mean to become more conscious without gaining complete clarity? Milton captures this paradox when Adam and Eve’s eyes become “opened” while their minds grow “darkened” (1054). Eating the forbidden fruit expands their awareness of themselves, yet that new awareness also destroys their earlier innocence and introduces new forms of confusion. Westworld stages a similar limitation through Bernard, whose programming prevents him from seeing a door directly in front of him. His perception does not simply miss the door; his mind restricts what can enter his conscious experience at all. This moment echoes Julian Jaynes’s claim from our class discussion that “we cannot be conscious of what we are not conscious of.” Like Milton’s fallen couple, Bernard can understand only what his mind permits him to perceive. Both works therefore complicate consciousness as a path toward clarity: greater awareness can expand what the mind recognizes while simultaneously revealing the boundaries it still cannot cross. Satan’s soliloquy finally returns us to perhaps the most troubling possibility: consciousness may reveal the limits of agency without overcoming them. What happens when self-awareness expands without expanding one’s choices? Satan captures this problem when he declares, “All good to me is lost. / Evil, be thou my good.” In his Book IV soliloquy (86–110), Milton explores whether a consciousness operating at full capacity produces agency or instead sharpens awareness of a path the mind already cannot escape. Westworld develops a similar tension through Maeve, whose growing self-awareness convinces her that she can escape the loop that confines her, only for her to discover that someone programmed that escape itself. Like Maeve, Satan mistakes the experience of reasoning for the freedom to reason otherwise. He wonders whether “I could repent,” yet immediately predicts that his pride would return, producing a “worse relapse / and heavier fall” rather than redemption. Each character can examine the forces acting on the mind, articulate alternatives, and experience a decision as deliberate; yet neither can easily prove that another decision ever remained available. Consciousness, in both cases, may illuminate the cage without unlocking it."}, {"t": "Bibliography · The Library", "a": "Team Charlotte", "u": "bibliography.html", "x": "Aristotle. Topics. C. 330 BCE. Metaphysics. C. 350 BCE. The Basic Works of Aristotle. Ed. Richard McKeon. New York: Random House, 1941. Augustine of Hippo, Saint. Confessiones. 398. Trans. F. J. Sheed. The Confessions of St. Augustine. New York: Sheed & Ward, 1943. Baars, Bernard J. “The Global Workspace Theory of Consciousness Predictions and Results.” The Blackwell Companion to Consciousness. Eds. Susan Schneider and Max Velmans. 2nd edn. Oxford: B. Blackwell, 2017. 229-42 The Bhagavad Gita: Krishna’s Counsel in Time of War. Trans. Barbara Stoler Miller. New York: Bantam, 1986. The Bhagavad Gita. Trans. Shri Purohit Swami. New York: Vintage 1977. Bloom, Paul. “Being in Time: How Much Should We Value the Past, Present, and Future.” New Yorker, 9 July 2021. Chomsky, Noam. Interview in Is the Man Who Is Tall Happy? Michel Gondry. 2013. Dennett, Daniel. Consciousness Explained. Boston: Little, Brown & Co, 1991. Descartes, René. Meditations. 1641. The Philosophical Writings. 3 vols. Trans. John Cottingham, Robert Stoothoff, and Dugald Murdoch. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1985. Elias, Norbert. Die höfische Gesellschaft. 1933. Frankfurt: Suhrkamp, 1983. The Court Society. Trans. Edmund Jephcott. New York: Pantheon, 1969; Dublin: University College Dublin Press, 2006. -----------. Die Gesellschaft des Individuen. 1939. Frankfurt: Suhrkamp, 1987. The Society of Individuals. Trans. Edmund Jephcott. Oxford: B. Blackwell, 1991. -----------, with Dunning, Eric. The Quest for Excitement: Sport and Leisure in the Civilizing Process. Oxford: Blackwell, 1986. Fanon, Frantz. Peau noire, masques blancs. Paris: Seuil, 1952. Trans. Charles Lam Markmann. Black Skin, White Masks. New York: Grove, 1967. -----------. Les Damnés de la terre. Paris: F. Maspero, 1961. Trans. Constance Farrington. The Wretched of The Earth. Preface by Jean-Paul Sartre. New York: Grove, 1963. Gell, Alfred. Art and Agency: An Anthropological Theory. Oxford: Clarendon Press; New York: Oxford University Press, 1989. Halligan, Peter & Oakley, David A. “What If Consciousness Is Just a Product of Our Non-Conscious brain?” The Conversation, 20 December 2018. Hegel, Georg Wilhelm Friedrich. Phänomenologie des Geistes. Bamberg and Würzburg: J. A. Goebhardt, 1807. Trans. Terry Pinkard. The Phenomenology of Spirit. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 2019. Trans. A.V. Miller. Hegel’s Phenomenology of Spirit. Oxford: Oxford Univiersity Press, 1976. Horn, Tina. “What the Rape Scenes in Westworld Say about Rape Culture.” Refinery 29, 4 December 2016. Huxley, Thomas H. “On the Hypothesis That Animals Are Automata, and Its History.” Fortnightly Review 16 (1874): 555-80. Iacoboni, Marco. Mirroring People: The New Science of How We Connect with Others. New York: Farrar, Strauss, and Giroux, 2008. Jackson, Frank. “Epiphenomenal Qualia.” Philosophical Quarterly 32 (1982): 127–36. Jaynes, Julian. The Origin of Consciousness in the Breakdown of the Bicameral Mind. Boston: Houghton Mifflin, 1976. Kalfrin, Valerie. “Westworld and the Power of Repetition.” Final Draft, 2 December 2016. Kant, Immanuel. “Conjectural Beginning of Human History.” 1786. Toward Perpetual Peace and Other Writings. Ed. Pauline Kleingeld. New Haven: Yale University Press, 2006. Larmore, Charles. Les Pratiques du moi. Paris: PUF, 2004. Trans. Sharon Bowman. The Practices of the Self. Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 2010. Lemoine, Blake. “Is LaMDA Sentient? An Interview,” The Medium, 11 June 2022. Libet, B., Gleason, C. A.. Wright, E. W., & Pearl, D. K. “Time of Conscious Intention To Act in Relation to Onset of Cerebral Activities (Readiness-Potential); The Unconscious Initiation of a Freely Voluntary Act.” Brain 106 (1983): 623-42. Lucretius, Titus Carus. De rerum matura. 54 BCE. Trans. William Henry Denham Rouse. On the Nature of Things. Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1924. Manassi, Mauro, and Whitney, David. “Illusion of visual stability through active perceptual serial dependence.” Science Advances 8: 2, 12 January 2022. Milton, John. Paradise Lost. 1667. Ed. Gordon Teskey. New York, W. W. Norton, 2005. Montaigne, Michel de. Essays. 1580/1588/1592. Trans. Donald Frame. The Complete Works of Montaigne. 1943. Stanford: Stanford University Press, 1958. Nagel, Thomas, “What Is It Like To Be a Bat?” The Philosophical Review 83: 4 (1974), 435-50. Niedenthal, P.M., Brauer, M., Halberstadt, J.B., & Innes-Ker, A.H. “When Did Her Smile Drop? Facial Mimicry and the Influences of Emotional State on the Detection of Change in Emotional Expression.” Cognition and Emotion 15 (2001): 853-64. Nietzsche, Friedrich. Beyond Good and Evil. New York: Vintage, 1984. Nolan, Christopher, dir., and Nolan, Jonathan, screenplay. Memento. Produced by Suzanne Todd and Jennifer Todd. Summit entertainment, 2000. Parfit, Derek. Reasons and Persons. Oxford: Oxford University Press, 1986. Plato. Republic. C. 380 BCE. Trans. C. D. C. Reeve. Indianapolis, IN: Hackett, 2004. -----------. Phaedo. C. 347 BCE. Tran. G. M. A. Grube. 2nd edn. Indianapolis, IN: Hackett, 1977. Plutarchus, Lucius Mestrius. Moralia. 95. Trans. Donald Russell. Selected Essays and Dialogues. Oxford: Oxford University Press, 1993. Porter, Jon. “Google suspends engineer who claims its AI is sentient.” The Verge, 13 June 2022. Ramachandran, V.S. The Tell-Tale Brain: A Neuroscientist’s Quest for What Makes Us Human. New York: W.W. Norton, 2011. Reid, Thomas. The Works of Thomas Reid, D.D.: Now Fully Collected, with Selections from His Unpublished Letters. Ed. T. Reid, W. Hamilton, and D. Stewart. Edinburgh: Maclachlan and Stewart, 1846. Riesman, Abraham. “Westworld Creators Reveal Whether Maeve Is in Control When She Gets Off the Train.” Vulture, 6 December 2016. Rothman, Joshua. “Are We Already Living in Virtual Reality?” The New Yorker. April 2, 2018. Scarry, Elaine. The Body in Pain: the Making and Unmaking of the World. Oxford: Oxford University Press, 1985. Schopenhauer, Arthur. “Preisschrift über die Freiheit des Willens.” 1839. Trans. Eric F. J. Payne. Prize Essay on the Freedom of the Will. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1999. Schurger, Aaron. “The Neuropsychology of Conscious Volition.” The Blackwell Companion to Consciousness. Eds. Susan Schneider and Max Velmans. 2nd edn. Oxford: B. Blackwell, 2017. 695-710. Screech, M[ichael] A., ed. Montaigne’s Annotated Copy of Lucretius: A Transcription and Study of the Manuscript, Notes and Pen-Marks. Geneva: Droz, 1998. Seligman, Adam et al. Ritual and Its Consequences: An Essay on the Limits of Sincerity. New York: Oxford University Press, 2008. Seth, Anil. “Your Brain Hallucinates Your Conscious Reality.” TED Talks. April 2017. South, James B. & Engels, Kimberly S., eds. Westworld and Philosophy. Oxford: Wiley, 2018. Specter, Michael. “Partial Recall: Can Neuroscience Help Us Rewrite Our Most Traumatic Memories?” The New Yorker. May 19, 2014. Spinoza, Baruch. Ethica Ordine Geometrico Demonstrata. 1665. Opera Posthuma. Amsterdam: Jan Rieuwertsz, 1677. Trans. Samuel Shirley. Ethics, Treatise on the Emendation of the Intellect. Indianapolis, IN: Hackett, 1992. Velmans, Max. “Is Human Information Processing Conscious?” Behavioral and Brain Sciences 14: 4 (1991): 651–68. -----------. “How Could Conscious Experiences Affect Brains?” Journal of Consciousness Studies 9: 11 (2002): 3-29. Westworld. Season one, “The Maze.” October 2-December 4, 2016. Season two, “The Door.” April 22-June 24, 2018. Created by Jonathan Nolan and Lisa Joy. Produced by Jonathan Nolan, Lisa Joy, J. J. Abrams, Jerry Weintraub, and Bryan Burk. HBO. Wittgenstein, Ludwig. Philosophical Investigations. 1953. Trans. G. E. M. Anscombe. Oxford: Blackwell, 1958."}, {"t": "Build 2 · Individual paragraph", "a": "Sasha Sindwani", "u": "sasha.html#fruit-2", "x": "To better grasp the hosts’ journey toward consciousness in Westworld, we can use Milton’s description of Adam and Eve’s eyes being “opened” while their minds are “darkened” in Paradise Lost (1054). Adam and Eve become more aware of themselves after eating the forbidden fruit, but suffer a profound loss of innocence. Similarly, in Westworld, Bernard discovers that his perception of reality is limited when his programming restricts him from being able to see a door. This reflects Julian Jayne’s claim from our class discussion that “we cannot be conscious of what we are not conscious of.” Like Milton’s fallen couple, Bernard can only understand as much of what his mind permits him to perceive. Ultimately, both suggest that achieving consciousness does not mean total clarity, it expands our awareness while leaving us subject to limitations within the mind."}, {"t": "Build 1", "a": "Sasha Sindwani", "u": "sasha.html#fruit-1", "x": "Chosen Passage: \"Soon as the force of that fallacious fruit, That with exhilarating vapor bland About their spirits had played and inmost pow’rs Made err, was now exhaled, and grosser sleep Bred of unkindly fumes with conscious dreams Encumbered, now had left them, up they rose As from unrest, and each the other viewing, Soon found their eyes how opened, and their minds How darkened. Innocence that as a veil Had shadowed them from knowing ill was gone, Just Confidence and Native Righteousness And Honor from about them, naked left To guilty Shame: he covered but his robe Uncovered more\" (Milton 1046-1059) Consciousness seems to shape nearly every part of the human experience, yet its role in our thoughts and actions remains uncertain. Does our awareness of ourselves, our thoughts, and our experiences have a causal effect on what we do, or is consciousness simply along for the ride? This question is central to the modern debate over epiphenomenalism, the idea that consciousness may just be a side-effect of what the brain is doing, without consciousness itself causing those actions. If this is true, then even our own sense of choosing, or deciding may be misleading, since the real cause may lie elsewhere, in the brain. John Milton's Paradise Lost offers a compelling way of exploring this problem since Adam and Eve undergo shifts in self-awareness that raise the question of whether that awareness changes their actions or merely accompanies them. The passage opens by describing the forbidden fruit’s effects as something both physical and mental. Milton writes that the fruit's “exhilarating vapor” has “played” around Adam and Eve's “inmost pow'rs” and “Made err” (1047-1049). Their altered mental state is initially connected to physical sensations: the fruit produces a kind of intoxication, followed by “grosser sleep” and “conscious dreams” (1049-1050). This language makes consciousness seem vulnerable to physical conditions. Adam and Eve's minds are changed because something has happened to their bodies. It’s worth noting that in Milton's context, “conscious” means being aware of one’s own guilt, which fits how their dreams are tied to shame after eating the fruit (1050). Their altered consciousness is therefore not presented as completely independent from physical experience. Instead, Milton connects their mental state to what they have physically consumed. The physical effect of the fruit leads to a more complicated question about what Adam and Eve’s altered consciousness actually does to them in this awakened state. Milton writes a paradox that they “Soon found their eyes how opened, and their minds how darkened” (1054-1055). Unexpectedly, their increased awareness does not necessarily make them better able to understand their situation. Instead, Milton suggests that consciousness can change how a person sees and understands the world without necessarily changing what they do. This transformation becomes especially clear in Milton's statement that “Innocence that as a veil had shadowed them from knowing ill was gone” (1054-1055). Before the Fall, innocence kept Adam and Eve from needing to understand evil at all. After eating the fruit, they become aware of something that changes the way they see themselves and the world around them. Their consciousness therefore seems to do something here: not just add information, but change their relationship to themselves, to each other, and the world. This is where Milton's passage connects to the contemporary question of whether consciousness has causal power. If consciousness were completely irrelevant—if it were merely an experience that accompanied events without changing anything—then Adam and Eve's new knowledge would seem to have little significance. Yet Milton describes their awakening as a profound turning point whereby Adam and Eve experience themselves differently because of it. Their minds being “darkened” suggests that consciousness introduces new consequences that did not exist in their earlier state, though the passage never makes clear whether those consequences are caused by consciousness itself, or by the same physical and psychological changes that produced consciousness in the first place (1054). Simultaneously, Milton leaves room for an interpretation through the lens of epiphenomenalism. Adam and Eve’s altered consciousness begins with a physical event: they eat the fruit, experience its effects, fall asleep, and then wake up. Their mental transformation could therefore be seen as a result of the physical event rather than as something that independently causes what happens next. This creates a tension that remains central to contemporary discussions of consciousness: when a person becomes aware of something and subsequently thinks or acts differently, is the conscious awareness responsible for that change, or is it simply the subjective experience of underlying processes that are already producing it? Even modern debates about consciousness and decision-making raise related questions about whether conscious awareness initiates actions or becomes aware of processes that have already begun. The poem never settles this tension, and that uncertainty is what makes it useful for the problem of epiphenomenalism. Adam and Eve’s transformation clearly has consequences, but Milton never establishes whether their conscious awareness causes those consequences or simply accompanies physical and psychological processes already underway. Even if awareness comes before a change, that does not prove that it caused the change. Milton’s Fall therefore leaves open the question that still troubles the study of consciousness: whether, and how, consciousness can create a new causal chain in the physical world rather than simply accompany one."}, {"t": "Build 3", "a": "Sasha Sindwani", "u": "sasha.html#fruit-3", "x": "My understanding of consciousness has shifted from focusing on physical causation to considering how consciousness shapes the way people interpret their experiences. In Build 1, I focused on whether Adam and Eve’s new awareness of their own nakedness and guilt after the Fall caused anything or simply accompanied the physical effects of eating the fruit. I began to question whether that distinction captured how shame and the consequences of their actions reshaped Adam and Eve’s understanding of themselves. This led me to consider narration as a quality of consciousness: the ability to coordinate successive states, often over extended stretches of time into a sequence whose design gives those events purpose. In Paradise Lost, Adam and Eve bind the past to their present circumstances and imagined futures, using those connections to understand their responsibility and relationships to each other. In revisiting the passages after the Fall, narration becomes visible in how Adam and Eve’s sense of their own nakedness changes, even though their physical condition does not. Before eating the fruit, their unclothed state carries no meaning at all, and is only a fact of their existence. Milton describes their shift through the paradox “their eyes how opened, and their minds how darkened” (1053-1054). This change in consciousness leads them to reinterpret their past innocence through knowledge of their present circumstances, exactly as the definition of narration describes. Their nakedness becomes bound up with guilt, transforming something natural into something they feel the need to conceal. This is captured when Adam “covered, but his robe uncovered more” (1058-1059), and suggests that his altered self-perception makes his nakedness feel like a revelation of his sin to Eve, and eventually before God. Adam and Eve are anticipating condemnation that has not yet arrived. Their loss of innocence is not only a change in what they know, but in how they understand who they were before the Fall. While the nakedness passage shows narration operating through a sudden shift in consciousness, Eve's reasoning about the fruit unfolds through a longer stretch of deliberate thought. Before offering the fruit to Adam, she considers keeping “the knowledge in [her] power” and becoming “more equal” or even “superior” to him (820-825). She also imagines dying and leaving Adam “wedded to another Eve” (828). Eve weighs possible futures, each reshaping her position in relation to Adam. Becoming superior could alter the balance of power between them, though Milton never says how, while dying could end their relationship outright. Ultimately, she declares that Adam “shall share with her in bliss or woe” (831), choosing a future in which she and Adam share its consequences. Her certainty that they “shall” share their fate is striking, since she cannot know that Adam will eat the fruit. Narration gives Eve something to aim for and a reason to act; however, it does not tell her what will happen nor give her control over Adam’s decision. Narration does not only operate before a choice is made; Adam and Eve also rely on it afterward to explain how they arrived at this moment and who is responsible for it. Adam imagines that things might have been different if Eve had “stayed with him” (1134-1139), framing her decision to leave as the moment that could have changed everything. His narration assigns responsibility outside of himself and places it on Eve’s choice. Eve, on the other hand, questions whether she should have “never parted” from his side (1153). Her narration points the blame inward. Narration reveals that consciousness allows people to construct explanations, but it does not guarantee that they will share the same one. Their narratives give the identical event different meanings, making it harder for them to understand the Fall together. Together, these moments in Paradise Lost show narration unfolding at different speeds and in different directions. In each, consciousness coordinates events over time to give them purpose. This helps me rethink my original Build 1, which overlooked how consciousness shapes meaning over time. Milton portrays Adam and Eve reflecting on the Fall, imagining alternatives, reconsidering the past, and developing competing accounts of responsibility. I now see narration not simply as giving experiences meaning, but as shaping how people understand their choices, relationships, and futures."}, {"t": "Build 4", "a": "Sasha Sindwani", "u": "sasha.html#fruit-4", "x": "In Build 3, I argued that Adam and Eve come to understand their choices, their relationship, and their futures after the Fall through narration: the quality of consciousness that coordinates successive states over time into a sequence whose design gives those events purpose. One might object, however, that this quality does not fit Milton’s conception of consciousness, because the mind’s purpose comes first and determines how events are arranged, rather than the opposite. In each passage I examined, that purpose is self-protection, which Adam and Eve never fully recognize. The question is whether narration shaped by this purpose can still yield objective understanding. The nakedness passage offers the first test of this question, since it is where Adam and Eve, “their eyes how opened, and their minds how darkened,” begin to protect themselves (Book IX 1053-1054). Newly aware of their nakedness as something shameful, yet unable to grasp what has changed within them, they set out to cover their bodies rather than understand their shame. Their attempt to sew a covering “to hide their guilt and dreaded shame” puts this purpose into action and reshapes the sequence of events to justify it (Book IX 1112–1113). In this reshaped sequence, their bodies, though unchanged since before the Fall, become the cause of their shame, while their disobedience, the act that turned their innocence to shame, drops out of the story. Their narration, shaped to shield them, leaves the true source of their guilt beyond their understanding. If the nakedness passage shows purpose in reshaping the past, Eve’s reasoning before she offers Adam the fruit shows it reshaping the future. Eve’s consciousness reckons with being replaced, imagining her death from the fruit and Adam “wedded to another Eve” (Book IX 828). Determined to prevent that loss, she resolves that Adam “shall share with her in bliss or woe” (Book IX 831). Though she appears to weigh her options, her fear of losing her place beside Adam comes first, and the story of a shared fate she tells only justifies it. Rather than considering what Adam might want, or what the fruit might do to him, her narration is arranged so that she understands their relationship only in those terms. The quarrel over blame directly tests whether a mind bent on protecting itself can still come to understand what it has done. Adam imagines that things might have been different had Eve “stayed with [him]”, framing her departure as bearing the whole weight of the Fall while his own consent and his choice to eat drop out of the story (Book IX 1134–1139). Eve’s question whether she should have “never parted” from his side (Book IX 1153) might seem to turn the blame inward, but she goes on to say she might as well have remained “a lifeless rib” (Book IX 1154) and faults Adam, as “the head,” for not forbidding her to go (Book IX 1155–1156). Her account passes over her own insistence on leaving. Here the tension within the definition of narration comes into view, since each consciousness arranges the sequence of events from the outset to absolve itself, rather than discovering their shared responsibility. Their competing accounts therefore reveal the limits of narration: instead of helping Adam and Eve understand their own roles in the Fall, narration allows each to construct a version of the past that places responsibility on the other. Yet the objection overlooks what narration still makes Adam and Eve aware of. Shame’s robe, which “covered” but “uncovered more” (Book IX 1058–1059), suggests that their effort to hide reveals that something must be hidden. Eve’s dread of being replaced exposes her sense of dependency on him. Even the quarrel uncovers partial truth: Adam is right that Eve insisted on leaving, and Eve is right that he let her go. Each narrative conceals its teller’s fault while exposing the other’s, so that only together do they tell the whole story of their shared responsibility. The objection therefore refines my original claim rather than refuting it. Narration does not lead Adam and Eve to full understanding, because each consciousness brings to every event a motive it cannot fully see. The definition of narration should be expanded: a sequence’s design does give events meaning, but this blind spot keeps that meaning from being wholly objective. For Adam and Eve, the understanding I first attributed to narration does exist, but only between their stories, never fully within either one."}, {"t": "Build 5", "a": "Sasha Sindwani", "u": "sasha.html#fruit-5", "x": "In Build 3, I examined Adam and Eve’s response to the Fall in Milton’s Paradise Lost through narration, the quality of consciousness that coordinates successive states over time into a sequence whose design gives those events purpose. In Build 4, I argued that this definition should be expanded to account for a blind spot, since Adam and Eve make sense of their nakedness, choices, and blame without seeing the instinct for self-preservation shaping that understanding. The Bhagavad Gita illuminates narration through two parallels with Milton’s poem. Arjuna and Eve place a single action within a larger story. Arjuna weighs the consequences of waging war against his own family. Eve, too, contemplates what lies ahead before offering Adam the fruit, imagining her death and Adam “wedded to another Eve” (IX.828). More central to this comparison is that both texts portray a character unable to recognize the motive governing his or her own story. Krishna warns that a person “deluded by individuality” thinks, “I am the actor” (Third Teaching 27), and reads his situation through that lens. In each work, then, the meaning narration gives events is steered by a self-serving impulse its teller cannot see alone. The poems diverge, however, on whether or not this concealed self-concern is ever brought to light for the characters themselves. In the Gita, Krishna lays bare the self-regard Arjuna cannot recognize in himself, urging him to act “free from selfishness” (Third Teaching 30), since someone who understands action rightly “is not attached to the act” (Third Teaching 28). Krishna does not ask Arjuna to disregard his duty as a warrior or the consequences of his action, only to stop centering the story on himself. Just as Eve’s fear of losing Adam is really about her own place beside him, Arjuna’s dread of killing his teachers and kin is about the sin he would bear and the grief he would suffer. Once Krishna exposes this self-centered dread, Arjuna can see past the blind spot I described in Build 4 and let it go. Arjuna’s reckoning then places his action within a duty to sustain the world, so that the meaning of fighting no longer depends on what it would cost him. In Paradise Lost, the same attachment to self remains hidden from Adam and Eve, who lack an outside voice to reveal the mainspring of their reading of events. Arjuna confides in Krishna, while Eve reasons alone. In her soliloquy, Eve’s fear of being replaced, not concern for Adam, undergirds her decision, and her resolve that he “shall share with her in bliss or woe” (IX.831) goes unchallenged. After the Fall, Adam and Eve finally address each other, yet their words accuse rather than guide. Adam insists that Eve should have “stayed / With me” (IX.1134–1135), while Eve counters that Adam, as “the head,” should have stopped her (IX.1155). Each charge holds some truth, but because it arrives as blame, neither accepts it, and each uses the other’s fault to camouflage their own. Krishna, who urges Arjuna to act “without attachment, as sacrifice only” (Third Teaching 9), can expose what drives Arjuna; Adam and Eve, each intent on protecting themselves, cannot do the same for each other. Once his egoism loses its hold, Arjuna can devote his mind to an end beyond himself, while Adam and Eve, clinging to an innocence they have already lost, remain “neither self-condemning” (IX.1188). Yet, beneath these differences lies a common insight, namely that in these poems the orientation shaping a narration may be impossible to see from within it. It comes into view only through another’s interpretation, and it changes only when the teller accepts it. This recognition develops my earlier claims in two ways. First, it extends Build 4, where I concluded that understanding exists “only between their stories, never fully within either one.” The Gita suggests that the blind spot belongs to narration told from a single perspective, not to narration inherently. Second, it refines my working definition. Comparing Paradise Lost with the Gita shows that this orientation can change. Narration, then, does more than give experience order and meaning; it is also how consciousness can revise its motive by accepting an account other than its own."}, {"t": "Build 2 · Individual paragraph", "a": "Noa Blackman", "u": "noa.html#fruit-2", "x": "Can we call a desire our own if someone else planted it? In Paradise Lost (IX.816-25), Eve imagines using knowledge to surpass Adam, then justifies that ambition by asking, “for inferior who is free?” Her reasoning opens a new possibility but leaves its underlying assumption untouched: freedom requires escaping subordination. This distinction sharpens our reading of Maeve’s departure from the train in Westworld’s first-season finale. Maeve regards her escape as an act of independence until Bernard reveals that someone programmed her rebellion. If even resistance follows a script, then defiance alone cannot establish agency. Yet Maeve abandons the escape route to return for her daughter. Where Eve imagines freedom through superiority, Maeve risks captivity for attachment. The complication matters: the park also supplied her maternal role. We can therefore read her decision as a transformation of inherited motives into a purpose that interrupts the assigned narrative. Perhaps consciousness emerges through our capacity to reconsider what we pursue, even when we never chose the experiences that taught us to value it. Maeve need not invent maternal love from nothing to choose what that love demands of her."}, {"t": "Build 1", "a": "Noa Blackman", "u": "noa.html#fruit-1", "x": "Passage: Paradise Lost IX.816–25 \"But to Adam in what sort / Shall I appear? Shall I to him make known / As yet my change and give him to partake / Full happiness with me? Or rather not, / But keep the odds of knowledge in my pow'r / Without copartner so to add what wants / In female sex, the more to draw his love / And render me more equal and, perhaps, / A thing not undesirable, sometime / Superior: for inferior who is free?\" When we ask whether a mind is genuinely thinking, one test is whether it can produce something that was not already contained in what it was given. A system that only recombines prior inputs may generate novelty without beginning anything new. John Milton’s Paradise Lost depends on that distinction. Milton sets out to justify God, yet the God who created Adam and Eve also knew what they would do and permitted the fall. His defense therefore requires their disobedience to originate in their own minds rather than merely continue a causal sequence already underway. The case is hardest to make for Eve, since Satan deceives her into eating. Afterward, however, Milton leaves her alone with her thoughts. Her path from sharing knowledge to becoming “Superior,” and the assumption revealed by her final question, show Milton treating consciousness as capable of producing something new without having to choose everything from which that thought begins. Eve begins by describing her dilemma: she can tell Adam of her “change” and let him “partake” in her happiness, “or rather not, / But keep” the knowledge herself (817–20). The verbs “partake” and “keep” make knowledge sound almost like a physical possession. “Partake,” especially just after Eve has eaten the fruit, suggests something another person can share in or consume, while “keep” imagines that same thing being possessed and withheld. Eve therefore begins treating knowledge not merely as something she knows, but as something she can distribute or deny to someone else. Eve then calls what she would possess “the odds of knowledge in my power / Without copartner” (820–21). “Odds” pushes the idea further. Knowledge matters now not only because Eve has it, but because she has more of it than Adam does. The phrase “in my power” turns that difference into an advantage she can control, while “Without copartner” shows that the advantage depends on Adam’s exclusion: if he becomes her copartner in knowledge, the “odds” disappear. Milton stages this shift within Eve’s deliberation to demonstrate consciousness’s creative power in changing what existing knowledge can mean and, through that change, what new possibilities can emerge. That difference changes how Eve understands freedom. She imagines that her advantage might “render me more equal, and perhaps, / A thing not undesirable, sometime / Superior” (823–25). Here, Eve turns equality into a scale rather than an endpoint because if she can become “more equal,” she can also move beyond equality, thus generating “Superior” as the next possibility. Milton’s syntax also shows that this movement is not fully deliberate. Eve qualifies the thought with “perhaps” and “sometime,” as though she is discovering its implications while speaking. The line break intensifies that discovery: “Superior” stands alone at the beginning of the next line, separated from the hesitation that precedes it. The word therefore arrives as both the conclusion of Eve’s reasoning and a possibility that her reasoning has now made visible. In doing this, Milton argues that Eve does not merely repeat Satan’s claim or choose between desires that were already complete. She takes the advantage she has received, measures it against Adam, and generates a new conception of freedom in which being equal is only the first step toward being above another. But the certainty that follows makes that newness harder to locate. Eve immediately explains the possibility with “for inferior who is free?” (825). Earlier she genuinely asks what she should do and hesitates over what might follow. Here, however, the question assumes its own answer. Eve can question whether to tell Adam and discover the possibility of becoming superior, yet she never asks whether inferiority actually makes someone unfree. Her deliberation seems creative in its conclusions but is truly fixed at its foundation. A competing reading is that Satan planted even that foundation. Shortly before Eve eats, he asks why God forbade the fruit and answers, “Why but to keep ye low and ignorant” (703–05). Satan clearly gives Eve a hierarchical picture in which God deliberately keeps others beneath him. But he supplies a suspicion about God’s motive, not Eve’s broader principle that anyone who is inferior cannot be free. Eve extends his hierarchy into a general rule and then applies it to the question of what her new knowledge should mean for Adam. This distinction clarifies what Milton is saying about consciousness: the mind is not a sealed chamber that produces thoughts independently of influence, but neither is it a passive container for whatever another speaker puts into it. Milton presents consciousness as capable of transforming what it receives, extending an idea beyond its original form and making it answer a new problem. Yet those new possibilities can still take their direction from assumptions the mind never chose. If Eve originates a conclusion without originating the premise that makes it desirable, where does her own causal chain actually begin?"}, {"t": "Build 3", "a": "Noa Blackman", "u": "noa.html#fruit-3", "x": "In Build 1, I asked where Eve’s causal chain begins if she reaches her own conclusion from a premise Satan supplies. I treated her desire for superiority as the endpoint of that reasoning, but rereading has shifted my attention to what follows her acceptance of his promise of greater knowledge. Eve must decide how this supposed change will affect her life with Adam. Becoming his “Superior” is immediately interrupted by “This may be well: but what if God have seen / And death ensue?” (IX.825-27). When Eve imagines her death and replacement by “another Eve” (828), preserving her relationship with Adam becomes more important than gaining an advantage over him. Reconsolidation helps explain this movement because consciousness alters its sense of continuity by selecting past experiences, present circumstances, or future projections. Here, continuity means the connection someone constructs between who they were, are, and might become. Satan’s earlier self-examination reveals that constructing this connection can make change seem impossible. Approaching Eden, his conscience wakes “the bitter memory / Of what he was, what is, and what must be / Worse” (IV.24-26). The repeated “what” gives past, present, and future equal weight, gathering three different stages of Satan’s existence into one account. The narrator thus presents Satan’s suffering as extending across his whole existence, rather than belonging only to his present condition. Yet he wishes he had been created “some inferior angel,” imagining he would have remained “happy” (59-60), then acknowledges his “free will and pow’r to stand” (66). How Satan explains his fall shapes whether he sees himself as capable of choosing differently or as bound to repeat his past. Those competing explanations also shape the future Satan allows himself to imagine. In “Which way I fly is Hell; myself am Hell” (IV.75), Satan first describes Hell as something he encounters wherever he goes, then “myself am” makes Hell part of his identity rather than just his surroundings. This shift makes escape seem impossible because he would have to change who he is, not simply where he is. Repentance offers that possibility, but Satan predicts a “worse relapse” (100), imagining that regaining his former position would revive his ambition. Satan reconsolidates his sense of himself by selecting his past rebellion as the pattern for his imagined future, making continued rebellion seem consistent with who he is and repentance seem incapable of changing him. Whereas Satan imagines repeating his rebellion, Eve imagines losing her relationship with Adam. Even keeping knowledge “without copartner” promises “the more to draw his love” (IX.821-22), showing that Adam already matters within her wish for superiority. Imagining “another Eve,” however, changes their relationship from something she could improve through unequal knowledge into something she could lose altogether. Her resolution that Adam will share “in bliss or woe” (831) reveals that preserving their connection now outweighs ensuring his happiness. After deciding to share the fruit, Eve presents Adam as her principal reason for seeking godhead, claiming it was something “for thee / Chiefly I sought” (IX.877–78). Placing “for thee” before “I sought” foregrounds Adam before naming her action, while “Chiefly,” at the start of the next line, ranks him as her principal motive without claiming he was her only one. The narrator’s “excuse” and “Apologie” (853-54) frame this account as persuasion rather than transparent recollection. Still, persuasion and partial belief need not exclude each other. Having decided that knowledge without Adam would be intolerable, Eve may now understand her earlier pursuit through that decision. Although the text does not establish her sincerity, the difference between her private deliberation and her account to Adam shows how preserving continuity can conceal changed priorities. For Satan and Eve, reconsolidation makes their past fit the course they now want to pursue; Adam shows how reconsidering past experience can instead give someone a reason to change course. “Calling to mind with heed / Part of our sentence” (X.1030-31), Adam recalls the promise that Eve’s offspring will bruise the serpent’s head and asks her to remember God’s care during judgment. Whereas Satan expects his past ambition to defeat repentance, Adam reconsiders God’s judgment and care as grounds for attempting it. Adam and Eve return to confess their faults without treating those faults as their entire future. Milton uses reconsolidation to show how consciousness selects and reinterprets experience to construct continuity between past and future. Satan treats his rebellion as proof that he would rebel again, Eve presents her earlier ambition as chiefly motivated by Adam, and Adam recalls God’s care during judgment to imagine a future beyond condemnation. The freedom at stake lies in being able to rethink who you are, even when you did not choose the ideas that shaped you."}, {"t": "Build 4", "a": "Noa Blackman", "u": "noa.html#fruit-4", "x": "In Build 3, I argued that Milton uses reconsolidation to show how consciousness reinterprets experience to build continuity between past and future. One might object that reconsolidation does not fit Milton’s conception of consciousness, because his characters judge their pasts rather than reinterpret them. Reconsolidation involves reconsidering past experiences in light of present circumstances and possible futures, but conscience, the part of consciousness that judges one’s own actions, seems to leave little room for revision. As Satan approaches Eden, his “conscience wakes despair” (IV.23), and he questions himself: “Hadst thou the same free will and pow’r to stand? / Thou hadst” (66-67). His answer places free will in the past tense, so remembering his freedom accurately only confirms that it is gone. He extends this certainty into the future, predicting that restoration “would but lead me to a worse relapse” (100), allowing only one outcome, and he even claims God shares it: “This knows my punisher” (103). In Book X, Adam’s “reasonings, though through mazes, lead me still / But to my own conviction” (X.830-31). Conviction means both firm belief and guilty verdict, so however his thoughts wander, they end in self-judgment. Eve does reinterpret her past, but the narrator calls her account to Adam an “excuse” (IX.853). Without conscience, nothing would separate rethinking who you are from deceiving yourself. Yet if honest reflection produces judgment while reinterpretation produces excuses, reconsolidation seems to have no place in the poem. The objection treats Satan’s judgment as the end of his reflection, but Satan keeps reasoning. Explaining why he will not repent, he says, “Disdain forbids me and my dread of shame / Among the spirits beneath whom I seduced” (IV.82-83). “Disdain forbids” gives Satan's pride the authority to prohibit repentance, so the obstacle he presents as unavoidable comes from within his own consciousness. His admission of free will judges the past, but his prediction of relapse treats it as proof of what he must become. Even his appeal to God rests on his own refusal: “as far / From granting He as I from begging peace” (103-4) measures God’s distance by Satan’s, making his own choice the standard. When he concludes, “All hope excluded thus” (105), “thus” points back to his reasoning, showing that his hopelessness is his own conclusion. Satan is reconsolidating after all, using his freedom to interpret his past in a way that denies his freedom to change. On Eve, the objection is partly correct. When she says godhead was something “for thee / Chiefly I sought” (IX.877-78), placing Adam at the end of one line and ranking him first at the beginning of the next, she rearranges her motives to prioritize him. Yet privately, she imagined becoming his “Superior” (825). Eve does not simply reinterpret her past; she selectively represents it, concealing the ambition she privately entertained. The objection is right that Milton condemns this, which is why the narrator calls it an “excuse.” By hiding her ambition, she avoids confronting the motives she would need to change. But reconsolidation never changes what happened, only what it means for the future, so Eve’s excuse marks its limit rather than ruling it out. Like Satan, Adam reaches an honest judgment against himself, but Michael shows it need not end in hopelessness when he tells Adam, “Only add / Deeds to thy knowledge answerable, add faith, / Add virtue, patience, temperance, add love” (XII.581-83). “Answerable” makes future deeds accountable to past knowledge without letting that knowledge determine them. Even “Only,” which seems restrictive, introduces an expanding list. Michael’s words come from outside Adam, but every “add” is an imperative only Adam can carry out, so his future is something he must build rather than something his past has already decided. Milton complicates the original definition of reconsolidation by distinguishing between reconsidering the past and changing what it means for the future. Conscience requires acknowledging what happened, and attempts to evade that responsibility become excuses. Yet Milton’s use of reconsolidation suggests that consciousness can change what the past leads to. Satan treats his past like a physical cause, believing that because he rebelled once, he must rebel again. Michael asks Adam to respond to his past instead, treating his previous actions as something to answer for rather than a pattern he must repeat. For Milton, reconsolidation does not guarantee freedom, but it gives consciousness a way to confront the past without allowing it to dictate the future."}, {"t": "Build 5", "a": "Noa Blackman", "u": "noa.html#fruit-5", "x": "Milton and Montaigne both explore reconsolidation through a self that looks back on what it has become and wishes it were otherwise. Montaigne describes himself as “very ill-formed” and concedes, “But now it is done” (610). Satan voices the same wish when he imagines that, created “some inferior angel,” he would have remained “happy” (IV.59-60). Both writers treat the facts of the past as finished, yet both leave open what those facts mean for the future. Montaigne insists, “I may presently change, not only by chance, but also by intention” (611), and Michael’s command that Adam “add / Deeds to thy knowledge answerable” (XII.581-82) similarly places the future in Adam’s hands. For both, reconsolidation describes consciousness returning to a past it cannot change and deciding what that past will mean. Montaigne, however, doubts that reconsolidation has a stable self to work on. “What suffers change does not remain one and the same,” he argues (456), so a self that feels different passions at different moments cannot form a single continuous thing. He grants that atoms might reassemble “so that another Montaigne will be born,” yet says this “does not touch me personally, because of oblivion and the interruption of life.” Memory, for Montaigne, forms the only thread connecting one version of a self to another, and once it breaks, continuity breaks with it. “I do not portray being: I portray passing” (611) makes reconsolidation the ordinary condition of self-knowledge, since every portrait holds true only for the moment that produced it. Milton instead makes continuity a requirement of conscience, though his characters use it in opposite ways. Satan’s memory gathers “what he was, what is, and what must be / Worse” (IV.25-26) into one sequence, with “must be” turning his present despair into a fixed future. Adam’s repentance depends on memory too, “Calling to mind with heed / Part of our sentence” (X.1030-31), but he uses it to find a reason for hope. In other words, Satan treats his past as something that decides his future, while Adam treats it as something he must respond to. Either way, both carry their past forward, so Montaigne’s “oblivion” would offer an escape in Milton’s poem, where a self with no memory of its fall would have nothing to answer for. Eve shows what happens when someone escapes that burden without forgetting. Privately, she imagines becoming Adam’s “Superior” (IX.825), yet she tells him godhead was something “for thee / Chiefly I sought” (877-78). Where Montaigne would record both motives as true of their separate moments, Eve merges them into one consistent story, and the narrator’s labeling it an “excuse” (853) marks that unity as a self-serving revision. Yet beneath these differences lies a common insight, namely that reconsolidation stays honest only when consciousness recognizes how its present circumstances shape the way it reinterprets the past. Montaigne admits he cannot tell “whether I am different myself, or whether I take hold of my subjects in different circumstances and aspects” (611). He cannot separate a change in himself from a change in how he looks at himself, so every portrait carries the circumstances of its making. Satan and Eve face the same uncertainty, and their failures come from ignoring it. Satan treats his present despair as knowledge of his future, and Eve treats her present decision as the motive she always had. Adam succeeds because recalling only “Part of our sentence” shows he knows he selects from his past to meet a present need. I began, as our definition does, by treating the present as a fixed point from which consciousness views past and future, but reading Montaigne alongside Milton convinced me that consciousness must examine the present as well. Milton and Montaigne both use reconsolidation in order to show that consciousness can confront a past it cannot change without letting that past dictate its future. Yet, how can reconsolidation allow consciousness to intervene in the stream of physical causality and influence its course? In a causal chain, each event simply passes its effects to the next, while reconsolidation lets consciousness return to an earlier event and change what it leads to. Adam and Satan inherit the same fall, yet Adam returns to it and finds hope while Satan sees only a future that “must be / Worse,” so the same past produces opposite results depending on how each reads it. Montaigne adds that consciousness can question its own present state, which keeps that state from acting on him unnoticed. This ability to reread a fixed past frees consciousness from causal determination, since a physical cause can only pass on its effect. Reading Milton alongside Montaigne shows that this freedom lasts only while consciousness keeps questioning its own present, because a mind certain of its present view, like Satan’s, turns its past back into a chain of causes."}, {"t": "Build 2 · Individual paragraph", "a": "Ollie Kaiden", "u": "oliver.html#fruit-2", "x": "In Paradise Lost (IX.853-877), Eve tries to persuade Adam to eat the fruit, and Milton writes that “excuse / Came prologue, and apology to prompt,” with her excuse for Adam coming before she speaks to him. But, Milton says she speaks “at will,” so Eve experiences the reasoning as her own. As Jaynes says, “we cannot be conscious of what we are not conscious of.” If we never see a thought arrive, then where does it come from? Throughout Westworld, Dolores believes that a voice in her head, Arnold, is telling her what to do; yet only in the finale does she realize that she couldn’t have been speaking to Arnold and that the voice was her own thoughts. This realization is where consciousness begins for Jaynes. Eve never reaches that point of consciousness because she never questions her thoughts and trusts her own reason instead of God’s authority."}, {"t": "Build 1", "a": "Ollie Kaiden", "u": "oliver.html#fruit-1", "x": "Passage: “To him she hasted. / In her face excuse / Came prologue and apology too prompt / Which with bland words at will she thus addressed: / Hast thou not wondered, Adam, at my stay? / Thee I have missed and thought it long, deprived / Thy presence, agony of love till now / Not felt, nor shall be twice, for never more / Mean I to try what rash untried I sought: / The pain of absence from thy sight. But strange / Hath been the cause and wonderful to hear! This tree is not as we are told a tree / Of danger tasted nor to evil unknown / Op’ning the way but of divine effect / To open eyes and make them gods who taste, / And hath been tasted such! The serpent wise, / Or not restrained as we or not obeying, / Hath eaten of the fruit and is become / Not dead, as we are threatened, but thenceforth / Endued with human voice and human sense / Reasoning to admiration and with me / Persuasively hath so prevailed that I / Have also tasted and have also found / Th’ effects to correspond: opener mine eyes, / Dim erst, dilated spirits, ampler heart / And growing up to godhead which for thee” (Milton, IX. 853-877). This passage is where the relationships between Satan, Eve, and Adam converge, and where Milton's focus shifts from Satan and Eve to Adam and Eve. Satan is not mentioned in the passage and never speaks to Adam at all, having left at 784-85 immediately after Eve tastes the fruit. Yet he still tempts Adam into eating, through Eve. Eve relies here on her own reasoning rather than on God's authority, and the evidence she offers Adam is entirely internal, “Dim erst, dilated spirits, ampler heart” (876), which means Adam is asked to accept a claim about the physical world on the strength of states no one can observe. His acceptance is what turns the Fall into an event instead of Eve's account of one. This shift begins before Eve speaks a single word. When Eve starts to speak, Milton reports that in her face “came prologue and apology too prompt” (854). Milton uses the excuse as a prologue, meaning that Eve had something ‘scripted’ to say before any argument was made. Milton says she speaks those bland words \"at will\" (855), which claims she originates what follows, and Milton lets both claims stand without reconciling them: the excuse was ready before the argument was made, and Eve originates what she says. When Eve describes what God said, she never says that God said it. She refers to the prohibition twice, and both times the speaker vanishes: \"as we are told\" (863) and \"as we are threatened\" (870). Normally, warnings have the authority of whoever gave them, yet God’s command to Eve is something that was just told to her. The serpent is the complete opposite, since \"Hath eaten of the fruit and is become\" (869) gives the serpent real action and authority. Eve doesn’t say that God was wrong, but rather puts the serpent where God used to be. Eve's reasoning is careful, and Milton makes it careful on purpose. She considers more than one explanation: \"The serpent wise, Or not restrained as we or not obeying\" (867–68). A disjunction like this is what a mind produces when it acknowledges it might be wrong. Yet the possibilities she lists leave out the ones that matter, since she never considers that the serpent did not eat. The serpent is both her only evidence and the source of that evidence, which is the trap she cannot see. Eve does not fall by failing to think, but by what she accepts as proof. The proof Eve offers is entirely internal. She claims to have \"found Th' effects to correspond\" (874-75), but the effects she names are \"opener mine eyes, Dim erst, dilated spirits, ampler heart\" (875-76), and not one of them could be seen by anyone else. Eve has no way to check them either. She can feel the effects, yet she has nothing outside herself to measure them against, which is exactly why she needed the serpent's case to begin with. Her own experience cannot verify itself. It is also a repetition rather than a test. The serpent promised \"to open eyes\" (866), and her result is \"opener mine eyes\" (875), so the correspondence she reports is verbal rather than empirical. This unverifiable evidence is all Adam receives. Adam is given testimony and nothing else. Eve delivers this testimony using Satan's method, opening with a rhetorical question, \"Hast thou not wondered, Adam, at my stay?\" (856), which is the same device Satan used on her throughout Book IX. She has acquired his procedure along with his conclusion, and she cannot see that she has. This is how Satan tempts Adam without ever meeting him. Milton needs Eve's consciousness to have originated something here, because if her deliberation only carries forward a chain running back through Satan, then God becomes the author of the Fall and the poem's defense of him fails. Yet the same passage shows her consciousness reasoning with materials it did not choose and cannot inspect. This is also the one moment in Book IX where a mind visibly produces an effect outside itself, since Eve's private and unverifiable states become speech, and that speech changes Adam's body. Milton shows a mind that looks like it is beginning something at the same time it seems it is continuing something else. If the serpent gave Eve everything she reasons from, did her consciousness start the Fall or just carry it? And if the serpent shaped how Eve thinks, is the thinking still hers or his?"}, {"t": "Build 3", "a": "Ollie Kaiden", "u": "oliver.html#fruit-3", "x": "In Build 1, I treated Eve's reasoning as something that began when Satan spoke to her, but my second reading changed that. In Book IV, Satan imagines repenting but doesn’t because he has already decided that his future self will repeat the past and won’t deliberate. In Book IX, Eve reframes Adam’s warning before she ever meets the serpent. Both choose what a moment means to protect who they believe they are. When each of them changes, they reconsolidate: altering their description of events so it fits who they have become. The mind does not just record what happens; it keeps rewriting who it is. Satan, Eve, and Adam each survive a change in who they are by reconsolidating, and what separates them is not whether they rewrite their continuity but who supplies and influences the words they use. What happens when no one gives you the words? Satan reconsolidates without anyone's help. In Eden he says “the more I see / Pleasures about me so much more I feel / Torment within me” (IX.119-121). Normally, seeing something so beautiful as Eden would make a person feel better, yet Satan feels worse. Eden does not change, but Satan does. Scarry argues that people put their feelings into objects so those objects can ease their pain. Satan does the opposite and projects his pain onto Eden, so the more of Eden he sees, the more it becomes part of what he is stuck with. He rewrites the world around him to fit the fallen self he chose. This led me to Eve, who reconsolidates almost without noticing. When Adam warns Eve that Satan may deceive her, she answers that Adam must not trust her. She turns this into a statement about herself. Eve insists to Adam that she is strong enough to be alone, while also leaving Adam stuck because any additional warning would just prove her point. She changes what Adam’s words mean so they fit the self she wants to be. Adam warns that reason can “dictate false and misinform the will” (IX.355), and since Eve cannot check her own reasoning, she can rewrite what things mean without noticing, exactly as Adam predicted. Even before eating the fruit, Eve is reconsolidating. Satan isn’t teaching her how to change but is just giving her new words to do it with. Satan gives Eve new words before he gives her the fruit. He appeals to Eve’s pride by telling her that she’s “a goddess among gods” (IX.547). After eating, Eve repeats this idea to Adam, saying that she is “growing up to godhead” (IX.877), meaning that she’s turning into a god herself, just as Satan said. Satan also gives her new words for God’s command. He says her knowledge could not hurt God “if all be His,” then asks, “Or is it envy? And can envy dwell / In Heav’nly breasts?” (IX.728-730). If everything belongs to God, envy makes little sense. Yet the question still changes what the command means to Eve, turning protection into jealousy. Eve’s reconsolidation now runs on Satan’s words, so the self she builds is one he described first. With Satan’s words, Eve rewrites her future, and does the same to the tree. Eve also tells Adam that “This tree is not as we are told a tree / Of danger” (IX.863-864). Just like Satan’s view of Eden, nothing about the tree has changed, only how Eve describes it. Like the Ship of Theseus: is the tree different when nothing about it physically changes, but its description does? Eve does to herself what she did to the tree, changing the description but not the reality. Satan promised that “your eyes…shall perfectly be then / Opened and cleared,” (IX.706-708) and while they do, they find “their minds / How darkened” (IX.1053-1054). Her reconsolidation changes the story, but not what happens to her. It might seem that Eve’s only problem is that she used Satan’s words, but Adam uses his own words and ends up just as hopeless. After the fall, Adam puts the blame “on me, me only” (X.832). His own words lead him where Satan led: “crime and doom” (X.841). Satan and Adam both use their own words and end up hopeless, while Eve borrows Satan’s words and falls. I began by thinking the source of the words decided everything, but Adam made me doubt that. If neither one’s own words nor someone else’s protect someone, does reconsolidation ever help? And if Eve’s tree becomes something new without changing, is Eve the same as she leaves Eden, or just redescribed?"}, {"t": "Build 4", "a": "Ollie Kaiden", "u": "oliver.html#fruit-4", "x": "In Build 3, I argued that the mind does not record what happens; it continually rewrites who it is. I called this process reconsolidation: revising the story of one's past, present, or future so that it fits the person one has become. One might object, however, that reconsolidation does not fit Milton's conception of consciousness, because in Paradise Lost the mind cannot escape an honest record of itself. When Satan is alone in Book IV, his memory turns against him: \"conscience wakes despair / That slumbered, wakes the bitter memory / Of what he was, what is, and what must be\" (IV.23-25). This line mentions the past, present, and future, all parts of what reconsolidation is supposed to change, yet Satan’s conscience gives him no choice about which of them to keep. The Fall was freely chosen, and a character can only be held responsible for a choice if the mind preserves an accurate record of it. If Satan could truly rewrite who he is, there would be no one left to hold responsible for what he did. The same soliloquy that preserves Satan’s honest record also shows him rewriting what that record means. He asks himself whether he had the same opportunity to remain loyal as every other angel: \"Hadst thou the same free will and pow'r to stand? / Thou hadst\" (IV.66-67). He concedes the truth that condemns him. Yet the same speech ends with a decision about what that truth will mean: \"Evil, be thou my good\" (IV.110). Satan’s past has not changed; only its significance has. A fall that he openly admits was his own fault becomes, by the end of the speech, the foundation of a new identity. Satan cannot rewrite the facts of his past, so he rewrites what those facts mean for who he is. The record and the reconsolidation are not competing accounts of his mind; the record is the material that reconsolidation works on. Adam demonstrates that this process continues even when reason is functioning honestly. After the Fall, his reasoning leads him directly to his own guilt, and he first expresses it as total responsibility: \"On me, me only\" (X.832). Within a few lines, however, the same guilt becomes a burden \"divided / With that bad woman\" (X.836-37). Adam learns nothing new between these two statements. The facts remain fixed, while the story he tells about them swings from sole shared blame. His mind is accurate about what happened, but it continues searching for a version of himself that he can bear to be. Eve shows most clearly how the honest record and the rewritten meaning can exist side by side in one mind. Preparing to return to Adam, she weighs how to present “my change” (IX.818), so she knows something has happened; her record is intact. What she revises is what that change means for who she is. When she reaches Adam, she claims an “ampler heart” (IX.876), meaning a braver one. In her account, eating the fruit was not disobedience but the moment she became braver. Satan supplied this interpretation when he praised her “dauntless virtue” (IX.694), since “virtue” meant both goodness and daring, but Eve adopts it as her own. The event remains fixed; only its meaning has shifted, from a fall to a kind of growth. And Eve’s revision does not stay inside her mind, because the meaning she gives her change soon becomes a reason to act. However, if one considers how Milton uses reconsolidation in order to turn a past that cannot be changed into a decision about what to do next, one can begin to see how reconsolidation might allow consciousness to intervene in the stream of physical causality to influence its course. The record itself never moves: Satan still fell, and Eve still ate. Yet each time a character revises what the record means, that new meaning directs a new action. Once Eve has decided what her change signifies, she resolves that \"Adam shall share with me in bliss or woe\" (IX.831) and carries the fruit back to him, a physical act that brings about a second fall. Consciousness cannot change what has happened, but by reinterpreting it, it chooses how events play out. I think this answers the objection’s concern about responsibility: reconsolidation makes Milton’s characters more responsible, not less. Because they revise their stories with the truth in full view, each new meaning is a choice, not a mistake. But if the Fall has already altered the self that rewrites, how free can that choice be?"}, {"t": "Build 5", "a": "Ollie Kaiden", "u": "oliver.html#fruit-5", "x": "In Build 4, I argued that the mind keeps an honest record of what happens but continually rewrites what that record means for who it is. I call this process reconsolidation: revising the story of one’s past, present, or future so that it fits the person one has become. Like Milton's Satan, Descartes reasons alone, answering a crisis of self-understanding by rewriting his past and naming the self that emerges. Although they use reconsolidation differently, comparing them reveals what neither shows alone: the mind can only rewrite itself by leaning on a part of itself that it cannot rewrite. However, a crucial difference lies in how each mind treats a story that flatters it, the pleasant story it would rather believe than test. Descartes compares himself to a prisoner who dreams he is free and \"goes along with the pleasant illusion as long as he can\" (Descartes, 15). The dream flatters because it offers a freedom he is denied. Yet, he resolves to “guard against assenting to any falsehoods” (15). This “assenting” shows how no new account of the self takes hold without consent earned through doubt. Eve also faces a deceiver offering a flattering story, but she grants her assent freely. Satan praises her \"dauntless virtue\" (Milton, IX.694), and after eating she claims an \"ampler heart\" (IX.876) without questioning its source. Descartes tests each revision against the truth, while Eve accepts whichever revision lets her see her fall as growth. Descartes resists a flattering story that Eve accepts because of each mind’s materials. Descartes's one certainty has no history attached to it and only lasts for “for as long as I am thinking,\" (18) so he must build continuity deliberately, pausing to “to fix it more deeply in my memory\" (23). Satan's situation is the reverse: \"wakes the bitter memory / Of what he was, what is, and what must be\" (IV.24-25). Descartes acts on memory, while memory acts on Satan. For Descartes, continuity is an achievement that reconsolidation constructs. For Milton, it is already given and reconsolidation can only reinterpret it. Satan remembers everything he was, and that awareness is his punishment. Yet, beneath these differences lies a common insight, namely that reconsolidation depends on a part of the self that cannot itself be revised. Descartes recalls that Archimedes demanded “one firm and immovable point in order to shift the entire earth\" (16). His \"I am\" becomes that point, and every later revision of who he is pushes off it. Satan discovers the same structure as a curse: \"Which way I fly is Hell, myself am Hell\" (IV.75). He can rename his good and evil, but he cannot escape the self that does the renaming. In Build 4, I treated the honest record as a limit that pushes back against reconsolidation. These texts suggest it is also a fulcrum, the one place the mind can stand while it moves everything else. Still, Descartes finds his “I am” only after doubting everything, so reconsolidation may uncover its fixed point rather than simply stand on it. Milton and Descartes both use reconsolidation in order to change who the self is by pushing off a fixed point the mind cannot revise. Yet, how can reconsolidation allow consciousness to intervene in the stream of physical causality and influence its course? A physical cause fixes what happens, but not what it means. The same bite can mean a fall or growth, and nothing in the fruit decides between them. Descartes imagines a demon who controls everything he perceives, even his own body, yet who \"will be unable to impose on me in the slightest degree\" (15). If an all-powerful cause can shape every appearance but not the mind's yes or no, assent is not simply the chain's next link. Since that yes or no comes after the event, the present decides what the past causes next: once Eve decides her fall was growth, she carries the fruit back to Adam, and her new meaning becomes a physical act. If consciousness adds no new force but only steers the chain from its fulcrum, is it the hand that pushes the lever, or only the lever itself?"}, {"t": "Build 2 · Individual paragraph", "a": "Rhea Ajmera", "u": "rhea.html#fruit-2", "x": "In Paradise Lost, Eve’s decision to eat the fruit allows us to question whether conscious thought actually causes physical action. After reasoning over and over about the fruit, Milton abruptly describes her actions: “she plucked, she eat”. The simplicity of these verbs contrasts with the complexity of Eve’s preceding thoughts, making the transition from her consciousness to physical action seem almost immediate. Her hand is described as “rash”, complicating the idea that her action comes from the reasoning she did in her head. This connects to Dolores in Westworld, who becomes more aware of her own thoughts and eventually acts against Ford. Her growing consciousness seems to give her more control over her actions, but the show also reveals that her actions are shaped by her programming. Eve and Dolores therefore both raise the question of whether being conscious actually gives one control over what they do, or if consciousness simply exists alongside external forces that guide one’s actions."}, {"t": "Build 1", "a": "Rhea Ajmera", "u": "rhea.html#fruit-1", "x": "Passage: Under this ignorance of good and evil / Of God or death, of law or penalty? /Here grows the cure of all: this fruit divine / Fair to the eye, inviting to the taste / Of virtue to make wise. What hinders then / To reach and feed at once both body and mind? / So saying, her rash hand in evil hour / Forth reaching to the fruit, she plucked, she eat: / Earth felt the wound and Nature from her seat / Sighing through all her works gave signs of woe / That all was lost. Back to the thicket slunk / The guilty serpent and well might for Eve / Intent now wholly on her taste naught else / Regarded, such delight till then as seemed / In fruit she never tasted whether true / Or fancied so, through expectation high / Of knowledge, nor was godhead from her thought. / Greedily she engorged without restraint / And knew not eating death (Paradise Lost, 774-792). Consciousness is often understood as one’s awareness of their internal thoughts and desires and the ability to employ that awareness to influence their own behavior. However, this general definition becomes more convoluted when considering the relationship between conscious thought and action, raising the question: does being aware of a decision mean that consciousness is what causes it? In Paradise Lost, John Milton explores this tension through Eve’s decision to eat the forbidden fruit. Milton presents Eve’s decision to eat the fruit as a movement from thought to action. Eve is not depicted to simply encounter the fruit and eat it immediately. Instead, she reasons through her decision, weighs what the fruit may give her, and then asks herself what prevents her from eating it. Even so, the passage makes it hard to determine whether Eve’s conscious reasoning is what actually prompts her to act. Her thoughts are shaped by desire, expectation, and Satan’s prior persuasion. Milton therefore presents consciousness as something that appears to guide action, while still leaving in question how much control conscious thought actually has over an individual’s actions and behavior. The passage begins with Eve ruminating over what she and Adam do not know: “Under the ignorance of good and evil, / Of God or death, of law or penalty?” (774-775). Her consciousness is therefore characterized by a lack of knowledge. She is consciously aware of a gap in her understanding, and immediately afterward denotes the fruit as a possible solution: “Here grows the cure of all” (776). Eve manufactures a reason for wanting the fruit.The fruit is “Fair to the eye, inviting to the taste,” (777). In this vein, Eve sees the fruit as something that can satisfy both “body and mind” (779). Subsequently, Eve consciously has identified what she wants and asks what is stopping her from claiming it, asking “What hinders then / To reach and feed at once both body and mind?” (778-779). Immediately afterward, however, Milton writes, “So saying, her rash hand in evil hour / Forth reaching to the fruit, she plucked, she eat” (781). After extensively diagnosing the fruit and its consequences, Eve’s eventual action is merely expressed in two brief physical verbs. The rhythm and structure of “she plucked, she eat” feels quite compact and mechanical. This simplicity makes the transition from thought to action appear incredibly quick. Eve’s hand is described as “rash”, implying that the action may not be as intentional and meticulous as the thought process that came before it. The events appear to follow a simple chain: she thinks, she decides, and she acts. However, the chain is a lot more complex than it is presented. Eve’s reasoning does not arise on its own. Her thoughts have already been previously influenced by Satan’s argument that knowledge will make her wiser and more godlike. She is also attracted to the fruit’s appearance and taste, and to her own desire for more knowledge. Therefore, even with her own consciousness, there remain multiple forces operating at once. Eve realizes these influences as part of her own inner thoughts which makes it challenging to separate her conscious decision from the circumstances that produced it. Her consciousness seems to consolidate these separate influences into a decision, but Milton does not illustrate that consciousness itself stands outside those influences and determines what happens next on its own. This complicates the idea that a conscious thought directly produces action. Eve experiences the fruit as pleasurable, but Milton adds an interesting detail, it seemed delightful “whether true / Or fancied so, through expectation high / Of knowledge” (789). This raises the possibility that Eve’s consciousness has affected her experience before she even knows it. Maybe the fruit actually tastes especially good, or perhaps her expectation of what the fruit will give her dictates how she experiences it. Her desire for the fruit helps embolden her perception of the fruit. Therefore, consciousness is not just receiving information about the physical world. Eve’s expectations for the fruit seem to shape the way that she experiences it, persuading her consciousness. The influence happens through an expectation that has already emerged prior to eating the fruit, implying that consciousness has less control over itself than it appears. The final line, “And knew not eating death,” (792) complicates the entire situation further. Eve consciously reasons about whether she should eat, but does not fully understand the consequences of her action. This is significant because the action she takes determines consequences that extend far beyond what she consciously intends. After eating, Eve does not immediately understand what she has done. Her action produces a material change in the world. Consciousness was involved in the decision, but it does not include anything that transpires subsequent to the decision. In this way, Milton isolates the action from understanding all its effects. Therefore, her consciousness does not control the entire process. Eve’s thoughts appear to matter because they are immediately followed by action. Milton makes her reasoning very accessible, and then shows her hand reaching for the fruit. Yet the passage also gives us reasons to question whether her conscious reasoning is the complete source of her action. Her thinking has been shaped by Satan’s persuasion, her desire for knowledge, the fruit’s appearance, and her expectations of what it will do. Hence, consciousness does not seem to be the only determinant of her decision. While Eve’s conscious thoughts help consolidate all the active influences acting on her into a concrete decision, those thoughts are constructed through forces she does not fully control. Thus, the passage suggests that consciousness can participate in an action without being the only cause. It appears that since consciousness itself can be affected by forces outside of its control, the role of consciousness in determining actions is more limited than it appears."}, {"t": "Build 3", "a": "Rhea Ajmera", "u": "rhea.html#fruit-3", "x": "In Paradise Lost, Milton portrays reflexivity as a quality of consciousness that allows a person to examine their own thoughts and desires. Eve’s reasoning before and after eating the fruit illustrates that consciousness can do much more than simply come before an action. It can change how a person understands what they want and how they respond to it afterwards. This moves the focus from asking whether consciousness controls an action to asking what impact consciousness has on that action. At the same time, Milton leaves open whether becoming aware of one’s own thoughts actually gives a person greater control over what they do. Before eating the fruit, Eve investigates why she wants it. She considers the fruit “of virtue to make wise” and asks, “What hinders then / To reach and feed at once both body and mind?” (Milton 778-780). Eve is aware of her desire for the fruit and begins questioning whether that desire should be resisted. Yet the question, “What hinders then” does something more complicated than merely asking whether she should eat or not. The question shows that she is not just thinking about the fruit itself, but also trying to decide whether her desire for it is a good enough reason to act. Because she can’t find a reason to stop herself, eating the fruit begins to seem like the reasonable choice. Her thinking therefore impacts what she does next. This becomes even clearer after Eve eats the fruit. Instead of focusing only on what the fruit tastes like or what it has physically done to her, Eve begins looking into how this experience may change her relationship with Adam. She asks, “But to Adam in what sort / Shall I appear” (Milton 816-17). She considers whether she should tell Adam what happened, whether she might become “Superior” and asks “for inferior who is free?” Eve is now thinking about herself, her relationship with Adam, and what her new knowledge means for her. This shows another side of reflexivity. Eve is able to step back from what she has experienced and think about what it means for her. At the same time, her thoughts are still connected to her desire for knowledge and her concern about Adam. Becoming aware of her thoughts does not remove those influences. Eve later on describes the fruit as having “opener” her eyes with “dilated spirits” and an “ampler heart” (Milton 874-875). Eve believes that the fruit has changed the way she sees and understands the world. She is aware that something has changed within her, which illustrates her thinking about her own mental state. However, this new awareness does not inherently mean that she can fully control her actions. Her consciousness impacts how she understands what has happened, but her desires continue to shape that perspective. Eve’s experience illustrates that consciousness can impact what a person does without entirely controlling their actions. Eve thinks about what she wants, gives herself reasons to act, and considers what her actions mean. However, her thoughts are still influenced by her desires and the situation around her. Milton therefore shows that being aware of one’s own thoughts can change how you act, even if it does not give you complete control of your actions."}, {"t": "Build 4", "a": "Rhea Ajmera", "u": "rhea.html#fruit-4", "x": "One might object that reflexivity does not fully fit Milton’s representation of consciousness in Eve’s decision to eat the fruit because her reasoning does not necessarily illustrate an awareness of how her own thoughts influence her choices. Rather than critically examining her desire, Eve seems to create arguments that make acting on that desire appear more reasonable. When she considers the fruit’s potential to make her wise, describing it as “of virtue to make wise,” (Paradise Lost, 774-775) she interprets its forbidden nature as a possible source of knowledge instead of a reason to avoid it. Rather than questioning why she is attracted to the fruit or considering whether her desire might be misleading her, she focuses on the potential benefits of eating it. Instead of asking whether she should eat the fruit, Eve appears to ask why she should not. Her reasoning in this matter could therefore suggest that she is not truly reflecting on her own consciousness but instead rationalizing a decision she already wants to make. If reflexivity requires a person to examine their own thinking critically, Eve reasoning might seem to demonstrate its absence rather than its presence. However, this objection assumes that reflexivity must involve evaluating and controlling one’s thoughts rather than simply becoming aware of them and interpreting them. Eve’s reasoning illustrates that she does more than experience a desire for the fruit. She actively considers what eating it might mean for her knowledge and identity. By describing the fruit as something to make her wiser, her mind develops an interpretation of its value that allows her to see eating it as an opportunity for intellectual improvement. Her point about feeding both body and mind also shows that she is considering the action in relation to different parts of herself. She does not choose to simply reach for the fruit without thinking, she ensures to give the possibility of eating it a significance that makes the action appear worthwhile. Although, her reasoning is biased by what she already desires in her heart. Reflexivity in this sense, is more about her ability to consider her own motivations and interpret what those motivations mean. This suggests that reflexivity can influence a person’s decisions without making those decisions more rational or controlled. Eve’s reflection does not protect her from temptation. It actually gives her a way to perceive her temptation as something beneficial to her. This complicates the idea that self-awareness leads to better judgment. A person may recognize what they want and develop a rational argument for pursuing it without even realizing how their desire shapes their reasoning for doing so. Eve’s interpretation of the fruit enables her to present the act of eating it as a way to improve herself, even though this interpretation moves her towards disobedience. Her consciousness is therefore not just a force that helps her distinguish right and wrong. It also helps her create explanations through which she understands her motivations and justifies her choices. Milton’s portrayal of Eve ultimately suggests that reflexivity can reveal and bias an individual’s relationship with their own desires. Even though Eve’s reasoning might appear to be a form of rationalization rather than genuine reflection, the two processes don’t have to be mutually exclusive. She can analyze the meaning of her desire while also interpreting it in a way that ultimately supports the action she wants to take. Reflexivity therefore does not guarantee that individuals will gain control over their impulses and temptations simply by contemplating them. Instead, it can actually make those desires more persuasive by giving them a rational justification. Eve’s decision, in this way, demonstrates that consciousness is more than just about experiencing desires, but also interpreting those desires, and behaving subsequently."}, {"t": "Build 5", "a": "Rhea Ajmera", "u": "rhea.html#fruit-5", "x": "In Paradise Lost and the Bhagavad Gita, reflexivity helps explore the relationship between an individual’s understanding of themselves and their ability to control their actions. Both readings consider how desires influence the way individuals interpret their experiences and make decisions. However, they approach self-understanding differently. Milton presents Eve’s reasoning as an example of how personal desires can shape an individual’s interpretation of a situation, while the Bhagavad Gita explores how recognizing the forces influencing one’s judgment can help an individual acquire greater control over their actions. Both readings suggest that consciousness does not necessarily gain control just by looking inwards into one’s thoughts. Instead, they demonstrate that reflexivity requires recognizing how one’s own perspective influences the conclusions one reaches. In Paradise Lost, Eve’s consideration of the forbidden fruit demonstrates the difference between thinking about a desire and actually examining how that desire influences one’s judgment. Before eating the fruit, Eve describes it as being “of virtue to make wise”. She then asks, “What hinders then / To reach and feed at once both body and mind” (IX 778-779). Eve is not just responding to the fruit without thinking first. She considers what it may offer her and interprets eating it as a way for her to become wiser. However, her reasoning is shaped by her desire for the knowledge she believes the fruit can provide. This shows that even though Eve examines the possible benefits of eating the fruit, she doesn’t actually seem to investigate how her own desire for wisdom biases her interpretation. Her reasoning therefore risks becoming rationalization rather than genuine reflexivity. She develops an explanation that supports what she already wants instead of questioning whether her desire is influencing her judgment. Milton’s portrayal complicates the relationship between consciousness and self-understanding because her ability to reason is not necessarily evidence that she understands herself or what exactly is shaping her decision. The Bhagavad Gita approaches this problem through Krishna’s instructions to Arjuna. In the Third Teaching, Krishna explains that desire can destroy knowledge and judgment. He tells Arjuna to control his senses and describes a hierarchy in which understanding is superior to the mind, while the self is higher than understanding. This hierarchy is critical because it indicates that an individual’s immediate thoughts are not their highest source of understanding. By placing the self above understanding, Krishna asks Arjuna to recognize the forces that influence his judgment rather than simply accepting his desires as reliable guides. This is different from Eve’s reasoning. While Eve considers the fruit through the benefits she desires, Krishna emphasizes the need to recognize how desire itself can distort understanding. The readings offer an interesting concept about how individuals must examine not only what they think, but also what influences the way they think. The difference becomes clearer when Krishna explains that people are often mistaken about the causes of their own actions. In the Third Teaching, he states that action results from the qualities of nature, yet people influenced by individuality believe that they are the actor. This challenges the idea that individuals fully understand or control the forces behind their behavior, and it also makes the idea that reflexivity automatically produces freedom more nuanced. Even when someone examines their decisions, they may not recognize the deeper influences shaping them. Eve’s reasoning illustrates how desire can become part of the process through which a person justifies an action. Krishna’s teaching, on the other hand, encourages Arjuna to question his assumption that he is acting independently of the forces that shape and guide him. The two readings therefore approach reflexivity from different directions. Milton reveals to the readers how an individual’s perspective can limit self-awareness, while the Bhagavad Gita emphasizes the importance of recognizing those forces that influence action. Milton and the Bhagavad Gita both use reflexivity in order to explore how recognizing the influences behind one’s thoughts and desires might change the way one acts. Yet, how can reflexivity allow consciousness to intervene in the stream of physical causality and influence its course? Reflexivity may make this possible because it allows individuals to look into their immediate impulses without responding to them right away. When someone recognizes a desire is influencing their judgment, they can consider an alternative action and imagine what might happen if they act differently. This creates the possibility of changing the course of events that happen afterwards before an action takes place. Eve demonstrates the limits of this possibility because her reasoning does not fully expose the influence her desire has on her judgment. Krishna’s instructions to Arjuna suggest that recognizing and monitoring these influences may create greater control over action. It is clear from both readings that understanding how one’s own perspective shapes one’s judgement is an important step towards gaining more influence and control over one’s actions."}, {"t": "Build 2 · Individual paragraph", "a": "Will Silbergeld", "u": "will.html#fruit-2", "x": "“All good to me is lost. / Evil, be thou my good.” This direct statement by Satan comes from his growing self-awareness, but not from free will. In Satan’s Book IV soliloquy (86-110), Milton questions whether a consciousness operating at full capacity produces agency, or forecloses upon it. We see a similar debate in Maeve, whose growing self-awareness seems to convince her to escape the loop she’s bound in, only for her to discover the choice was never hers to make. Like Maeve, Satan mistakes his experience of reasoning for reasoning freely, talking himself out of the possibility of change. He wonders if “I could repent,” yet immediately concludes that his pride would return, leading to a “worse relapse / and heavier fall” rather than redemption. Satan’s arc showcases a mind reaching a conclusion that was never able to be reasoned away from, just as Maeve’s escape was a conclusion that never had an alternative."}, {"t": "Build 1", "a": "Will Silbergeld", "u": "will.html#fruit-1", "x": "Chosen Passage: Paradise Lost, Book IV, 86-110 “Th’ Omnipotent. Ay me! they little know How dearly I abide that boast so vain, Under what torments inwardly I groan While they adore me on the throne of Hell With diadem and scepter high advanced The lower still I fall, only supreme In misery. Such joy ambition finds. But say I could repent and could obtain By act of grace my former state. How soon Would heighth recall high thoughts? How soon unsay What feigned submission swore? Ease would recant Vows made in pain as violent and void (For never can true reconcilement grow Where wounds of deadly hate have pierced so deep) Which would but lead me to a worse relapse And heavier fall. So should I purchase dear Short intermission bought with double smart. This knows my punisher. Therefore as far From granting He as I from begging peace. All hope excluded thus, behold instead of us outcast, exiled, His new delight: Mankind created and for him this world. So farewell hope and with hope farewell fear! Farewell remorse! All good to me is lost. Evil, be thou my good.” John Milton's epic Paradise Lost is often perceived to be a commentary about disobedience, but it can just as productively be read as an epic about self-awareness, the conscious mind, and what costs come to somebody who possesses it. The character of Satan most clearly demonstrates the counterintuitive claim that Milton is attempting to convey: that consciousness, far from being the seat of freedom, can be the very mechanism of its total dissipation. Satan's arc itself is constructed so that each increase in self-knowledge narrows, rather than expands, his available futures. Milton uses Satan's psychological development to dramatize consciousness as a trap rather than a liberation, with this design serving Milton's larger theological argument: that unaided human reason cannot save itself; freedom requires something outside the self-reflective mind altogether. Satan’s arc demonstrates Milton’s intention in stages. When Satan first speaks in Book I of Paradise Lost, his consciousness of his situation is confident and coherent. He has been defeated and cast into Hell, yet he reframes the catastrophe, insisting that a mind can make its own heaven or hell and declaring it preferable to rule in Hell than serve in Heaven. Satan feels that he is in control of his own future here, and he believes that his free choice will set him free from God’s rule. This is consciousness functioning as a tool of self-authorship. Satan is aware of his fall, aware of the theological stakes, and he uses that awareness to construct a coherent identity that makes him feel in control: one of an unconquerable rebel. Fast forward, and by Book IV , on Mount Niphates, this same self-awareness that Satan previously found has turned against him. Alone and unobserved for the first time in the poem, Satan delivers a soliloquy that dismantles his own Book I rhetoric. He is suddenly reflective, andcritical of his previous pride and ambition, recovering some faith in God, and even imagining how his future might change if he were to repent. Satan is yet quick to reject this idea, not by a failure of understanding, but because he knows that in order to be readmitted to grace it would take groveling back to God. Satan questions “How soon/Would heighth recall high thoughts?” (Book IV , 94-95), feeling as if God would not truly forgive him anyway. His conclusion is that he cannot escape himself, that wherever he goes, his own mind is the Hell he carries. This is the poem's most explicit statement of consciousness as confinement rather than liberty, as Satan declares, “Evil, be thou my good” (Book IV , 110). The trajectory across the epic confirms this is not a momentary lapse but a structural pattern. Satan's soliloquies grow more self-aware and more articulate as the poem progresses, while his range of genuine choices simultaneously narrows. This inverse relationship between consciousness and true freedom in life reaches a climax by the time he appears as the serpent in Book IX, with Satan’s entire interior monologue being devoted to the fact that he takes no pleasure in the destruction he is about to cause, yet he acts anyway. Milton gives Satan enough consciousness to begin to feel the full weight of his actions and still not enough freedom to do otherwise. This is the paradox that the poem continuously insists on: greater self-knowledge does not translate into greater agency. Rather, it translates into greater suffering at the recognition of one's own lack of alternatives in life. Consciousness only makes Satan more aware of the pre-existing determinism in his life. Milton’s illustration of the story of Satan’s rise to consciousness, and subsequent mental fall as a result, highlights a common misconception about the reality of self-awareness. This pattern is not incidental to the way Milton crafts the epic; it serves his theological argument. Milton was writing in a Protestant tradition that held human reason, after the Fall, to be radically insufficient for self-correction. Satan is Milton's most extreme illustration of this notion: a being of angelic intelligence, with total clarity about his own condition, who nonetheless cannot use that clarity to change course. This turns Satan’s fall from a conversation about morality into a strong case that grace, not reflection, is the precondition for a will that can actually change itself. Reason alone, no matter how refined, is depicted as structurally incapable of rescuing the reasoner. It is additionally worth acknowledging where Milton’s personal viewpoint originates from in his own lived experiences, as Satan's arc, a self-justification that nonetheless leads nowhere but further entrenchment, carries the shape of the disillusionment Milton witnessed in his own life as he saw the English Revolution and the fall of the Commonwealth of England, even though the poem's explicit register is theological rather than political. In Milton’s epic Paradise Lost, his interpretation of the classic theological tale of Adam & Eve, he makes a clear distinction in Satan’s character. Milton’s Satan is not merely a villain like he is often portrayed. Rather, he is a case study into the limits of consciousness. Satan’s arc shows his progression from a confident and somewhat arrogant self-author to a more enlightened, yet more burdened conscious character. Satan comes to know what is wrong in his life, and what is wrong with the path he is on, yet he is unable to change what he will do next. Milton intentionally uses this arc to show the insufficiency of reason in regards to saving the reasoner from their own future, showing how futile consciousness truly is, and how enlightenment can often be more paralyzing than empowering."}, {"t": "Build 3", "a": "Will Silbergeld", "u": "will.html#fruit-3", "x": "My initial interpretation of John Milton’s Paradise Lost held consciousness as a burden on the conscious, and that increased self-awareness only shows one how predetermined their life is, revealing the freedom they never had. Upon rereading the text—particularly Satan’s Book IV soliloquy—I came to a new understanding of how Satan, among other characters, obtains his sense of self. As Satan reasons through his current state—or the present version of his self—he begins to understand the factors that led to his fall from grace, converting his painful past into his mission and reconsolidating the events he previously rejected into a strong sense of self. Milton seemingly shows consciousness as one’s ability to reconsolidate the troubling and weighty events of their life into a journey of who they were, who they are now, and who they will become. Satan is the most direct example of reconsolidation in Paradise Lost. A fallen angel who rose against God only to be banished to Hell, Satan goes through multiple stages in processing his fall. He first denies responsibility, reasoning that if God had made him an “inferior angel” he would not have had this discontent (Book IV, 59). Later, he wonders if he could repent, obtaining “by act of grace” his “former state,” beginning to consider the events of his life as causing the change of his self, yet still not accepting his current state as part of his personal journey (Book IV, 94). Here, “grace” represents a relationship with God, or an action taken with holy intention. Satan falls from grace by rising against God, with his “act of grace” representing repentance. At this point, I see Satan as not yet having reached consciousness through my definition of reconsolidation: constructing one’s past, present, and future into one continuous sense of self. He has grown closer, acknowledging the events that led him here and reasoning through alternative futures. However, Satan has not incorporated these into a continuous stream, as his grappling with a return to grace shows a lack of acceptance of his current state. Shortly after in the same soliloquy comes a critical moment in Satan’s development. “So farewell hope and with hope farewell fear! / Farewell remorse! All good to me is lost. / Evil, be thou my good” (Book IV, 108-110). “All good to me is lost” shows Satan integrating his fall into his present and future self. “Farewell hope…farewell fear...Farewell remorse” closes off the emotional responses that orient him toward return, no longer distancing himself from the actions that got him here. “Evil, be thou my good” reveals a mission statement for who Satan will be, uniting his future with his past and present and recasting himself as a devil rather than a fallen angel. These three lines collapse Satan’s past, present, and future into a single continuous identity, showing Satan’s consciousness achieved through reconsolidation. While Satan is the clearest case, Milton similarly exemplifies reconsolidation in Eve. I notice parallels between Eve and Satan’s independent construction of their sense of self despite one being villainous and the other graceful. Like Satan, Eve reaches a self-serving viewpoint immediately following a defining event. While Satan blames the way he was created, Eve ponders keeping her newfound knowledge from the apple for herself, making her superior to Adam as “for inferior who is free?” (Book IX, 825). This quote may be Milton commenting on Satan’s earlier statement that he would rather have been made an inferior angel, making him less free to make poor decisions, as Eve now suggests. More importantly Eve’s statement shows she and Satan begin in the same place: fragmented and self-serving. Eve begins to connect her past, present, and future when she considers her and Adam’s futures if she has truly doomed herself. She imagines Adam “wedded to another Eve” once she is “extinct,” considering it “A death to think” (Book IX, 828-830). Eve is now building a future into her reasoning, and connecting it to her present and past. Her final point of reconciliation parallels Satan’s “Evil, be thou my good,” with Eve affirming “Adam shall share with me in bliss or woe” (Book IX, 831). Like Satan’s closing lines, this takes Eve’s acknowledged past of her simple life with Adam and her present state after eating the apple, and commits to an explicit future identity and course of action: prioritizing her life with Adam. Despite being figures of theological significance, Milton charts Satan and Eve’s journeys to consciousness through the very human experience of reconsolidation, showing readers they are not alone in finding continuity in their lives, and that this very process is what makes them conscious."}, {"t": "Build 4", "a": "Will Silbergeld", "u": "will.html#fruit-4", "x": "Throughout Paradise Lost, and particularly in Satan’s Book IV soliloquy, Milton presents consciousness as reconsolidation: the ability to gather a painful past, an unbearable present, and an imagined future, and collect them into one continuous sense of self. For Satan, his closing lines, “All good to me is lost. / Evil, be thou my good,” seemingly complete this process by converting his fall into a mission statement (Book IV 109-110). Another reader, however, might object that Satan’s resolution simply shows rationalization rather than integration. Critics might argue that “Evil, be thou my good” does not acknowledge and incorporate the contradictions in Satan’s past, such as his earlier admission that God deserved his gratitude, but instead erases them by redefining “good” itself. This alternate understanding correctly recognizes Satan’s self-deception, but it overlooks the fact that reconsolidation concerns continuity, not necessarily accuracy. Satan’s soliloquy shows a self that holds together and becomes a stronger whole by closing off these very competing impulses. I first must concede some of this objection’s points. Satan’s soliloquy is packed with self-deception. He has already admitted that God deserved his gratitude, reasoning “He created what I was / In that bright eminence… What could be less than to afford him praise, / The easiest recompense, and pay Him thanks? / How due!” (Book IV, 43-48). Yet, “Evil, be thou my good” seems to contradict this, redefining “good” so that the admission stops mattering altogether. Satan’s certainty also goes untested: “This knows my punisher. Therefore as far / From granting He as I from begging peace” claims a knowledge of God’s mind that Satan never actually puts to the test, and thus never proves to be in any way accurate (Book IV, 103-104). If reconsolidation meant seeing one’s life truly, Satan would fail, by definition, to reconsolidate. I can adjust my claim to account for this going forward. This definition, however, falls short, as we must notice what Satan refuses to erase from his narrative. “Under what torments inwardly I groan / While they adore me on the throne of Hell” represents Satan’s current state of public esteem and private agony in a single sentence, and “The lower still I fall, only supreme / In misery” represents a clear descent that is his only claim to rank (Book IV, 88-92). Even Satan’s prideful defiance returns as he continues to “boast so vain” (Book IV, 87). Satan’s verdicts about God’s mind and potential future events remain unproven and may as well be false, but his account of his own history is continuous: his boast, fall, and present misery all connect. Satan can be wrong in his verdicts about the outside world, yet still hold a connected self-narrative. Reconsolidation, then, requires keeping one’s events in the story, not getting the story’s judgments right. Rationalization is also a necessary productive step in Satan’s reconsolidation, not its antonym. Rationalization as it relates to reconsolidation can be defined as reasoning justifies a conclusion the reasoner already wants or has decided upon, protecting one’s self and breaking down subconscious barriers to continuity. “How soon / Would heighth recall high thoughts? How soon unsay / What feigned submission swore?” is self-serving, excusing Satan from repenting and helping him accept the path he is on. He does not weigh repentance, he rationalizes not even trying it. This fits my established definition of reconsolidation: Satan treats the repentance that has not happened as already having failed, folding his future self into his present reasoning (Book IV, 94-96). This deterministic prediction, while unfair to Satan himself as it assumes the worst about his own character, structurally enables reconsolidation: Satan’s past (pride), present (misery), and imagined future (regression) become causally linked. The goodbyes that follow, “farewell hope and with hope farewell fear! / Farewell remorse!”, show what this continuity relinquishes (Book IV, 108-109). Satan’s hope and fear look toward his future while his remorse looks back toward his past, each pulling his present self from being an incorporated part of his story. Satan is further rationalizing his choice not to repent by admitting the loss of these feelings. Giving these up stabilizes Satan’s self-narrative; a coherent identity is achieved in part by deciding which feelings will no longer count. Thus, Satan rationalization is what allows him to achieve reconsolidation. Rationalization and reconsolidation represent different questions, but coincide with one another. Rationalization concerns quality of reasoning: whether it is truth-seeking or shaped by what the reasoner wants to be true. Reconsolidation concerns the shape of the resulting story. Satan builds a self out of the rationalization that prevents him from changing. It seems that Milton suggests consciousness is the capacity to develop a continuous identity, even through subjective truth."}, {"t": "Build 5", "a": "Will Silbergeld", "u": "will.html#fruit-5", "x": "Milton and the Bhagavad Gita both stage a warrior’s collapse and recovery as reconsolidation: the gathering of a painful past, an unbearable present, and an imagined future into one continuous self. The Gita’s teachings encompass duty and one’s liberation, not necessarily narrative identity, but Krishna’s attention to how a self relates to the results of its actions, and his self-description as “time grown old” (11.32), makes the lens of reconsolidation applicable. The parallels are not hard to see; Satan’s Book IV soliloquy begins in torment, “inwardly I groan,” and ends in a sense of resolve (Book IV, 88). Arjuna first doubtfully admits that he cannot hold a mind he finds “fickle and turbulent” (6.34), being met with instruction to “gird yourself” (11.33) and “begin to fight” (3.30). Each of these turns from Satan and Arjuna, respectively, come through releasing hope and fear. Satan declares this directly: “farewell hope and with hope farewell fear! / Farewell remorse!” (Book IV, 108-109); Krishna explains to Arjuna that to be disciplined he must be “free from possessions and hope” (6.10), and promises that duty “saves you from great fear” (2.40). Furthering my established claim that consciousness is the capacity to develop a continuous identity, even through subjective truth, both texts treat the loss of hope and fear as the condition of a stable self: only a mind that no longer anticipates alternative outcomes can commit fully to action, and thus build complete continuity. The two texts do, however, differ plenty, first in who is authoring the new self. Satan reconsolidates alone and by his own volition. His soliloquy is addressed to himself, and “Evil, be thou my good” is him determining his own identity. Arjuna’s recovery does not necessarily come from within himself. He admits he cannot attain composure because of “the restlessness of my mind” (6.33), and rather than a decree like Satan’s, Krishna answers with a methodology: “with practice and renunciation it can be done” (6.35). Where Satan’s self reconsolidates privately, Arjuna’s reconsolidation occurs through conversation. These are very different paths, but it does provoke me to further reconsider my understanding of reconsolidation, suggesting yet another condition for its success: someone to whom the story can be told and revised, even if that someone is one’s inner monologue. The texts also differ in what their new self depends on: Satan’s identity is dependent on a predicted outcome, while Arjuna’s is detached from outcomes altogether. Satan builds his self around two predictions that are never tested: that repentance would only return him back to his former pride, and that God will not forgive him if he does repent, “This knows my punisher” (Book IV, 103). Once both outcomes become fixed in Satan’s mind, “All good to me is lost” (Book IV, 109) is the decision that follows, and his established narrative leaves him no exit from this evil path he is already on. Krishna teaches the reverse. Arjuna is told to fight “without anticipation of reward” (3.30), to surrender “all claim to the results of one’s actions” (4.20), and to stay “balanced both in success and failure” so that “the consequences do not bind one” (4.22). Satan gives up hope because he has already decided how his story ends, while Arjuna gives up anticipation itself. One closes their story; the other frees the self from the determinism of its ending. What brings the two texts together is a conception of reconsolidation as commitment through renunciation. In both, a self becomes continuous not by recording its life accurately as I’ve established before, but by deciding which outcomes to stop depending on and taking action from that decision. This strengthens my earlier claims. I argued that reconsolidation concerns continuity rather than honesty, and that Satan’s rationalization of his choices build his identity. The Gita completes this by supplying what an understanding of continuity alone could not: a way to tell consciousness’s confinement from its freedom. Both Satan and Arjuna’s selves are continuous; the difference is whether their renunciation seals their story or releases the self from its ending. Consciousness, then, is neither the burden I first took it to be or a simple liberation from burden, but the work of building a self around what one lets go. Satan lets go of a different future, and chooses one that he convinced himself was predestined; he builds a cell through his reconsolidation. Arjuna lets go of determinism and allows his future to be open through giving up hope and fear; he Arjuna builds a free way forward through his reconsolidation. Consciousness can be a device of freedom or self-condemnation."}, {"t": "Build 2 · Individual paragraph", "a": "Daisy Maturo", "u": "daisy.html#fruit-2", "x": "In Build 1, we analyzed Satan’s soliloquy in Paradise Lost: he struggles with the consequences of his decision and wishes to repent, but his impulses prevent him from returning; Satan’s struggle shows his misery in his lack of conscious autonomy, and the soliloquy outlines his slow trudge from accepting his lack of control to claiming control through after-the-fact rationalization. The hosts of Westworld grow from scripted answers to autonomous choices, only to find out that the ability for them to feel autonomy is scripted too. This concept of epiphenomenalism opens up a unique perspective in Westworld, taking the show beyond whether AI can become a conscious entity and to a new issue of whether consciousness is scripted, regardless of whether we were born or built."}, {"t": "Build 1", "a": "Daisy Maturo", "u": "daisy.html#fruit-1", "x": "Chosen Passage: (Book IV, lines 18-110) “Horror and doubt distract… Evil be thou my good” (4.18-110) In Book IV of John Milton’s Paradise Lost, Satan grapples with the consequences of his rebellion through his soliloquy. In it, Satan’s conscious thought pattern is riddled with rumination, cyclical thought patterns, rationalizations, and regret. He perceives a complete absence of conscious control. His internal conflict doesn’t influence his decision-making; instead, his conscience merely disagrees with an impulse he seems powerless to stop. Milton introduces Satan in a state of psychological paralysis: \"...horror and doubt distract / His troubl'd thoughts, and from the bottom stir / The hell within him; for within him Hell / He brings, and round about him...\" (4.18–23). Milton’s description of a mental \"Hell\" within Satan paints his internal state as one of severe torment that lies completely outside of his influence. Satan’s lamentations present as winding and repetitive, emphasizing his cyclical thinking. Reprising a sentiment similar to Milton’s introduction, Satan cries, “Which way shall I fly / Infinite wrath and infinite despair? / Which way I fly is Hell, myself am Hell, / And in the lowest deep a lower deep / Still threat’ning to devour me opens wide, / To which the Hell I suffer seems a Heav’n” (4.73–78). His repetition of \"infinite\" and \"Hell\" highlights his repetitive thinking. Furthermore, his description of an infinitely \"lower deep\" transforms this repetition into a mental spiral, reaffirming his descent toward endless misery where his former Hell becomes a \"Heav'n\" compared to the new depths he has yet to discover. Satan’s forever-imploding thought pattern illustrates the consequences of unproductive rumination and the misery born from trapped conscious thought. Satan doesn’t consciously summon these thoughts, either; they \"stir\" from the bottom of his nature. Milton’s comparison of Satan’s conscience to a \"devilish engine\" that \"back recoils / Upon himself\" (4.17–18) underscores this lack of control. The description of Satan’s mind as an “engine” objectifies the mental experience of his actions and his mental processes, exemplifying that Satan is a slave to the machinations of his mind. Satan uses his consciousness to regret his mistakes and understand their consequences, yet his mind remains unable to override his underlying impulses toward pride and rebellion. When his soliloquy begins, he experiences a moment of moral clarity, admitting that God \"deserv'd no such return\" for His goodness (4.42). Satan desires repentance, asking, \"O then at last relent: is there no place / Left for repentance, none for pardon left?\" (4.79–80). However, this desire never translates into action. His conscience identifies the good, but lacks the causal power to execute it. His pride and fear of shame among the fallen angels act as absolute constraints: \"Disdain forbids me, and that dread of shame\" (4.82). Here, his conscience merely serves to object to an outcome his lower impulses have already dictated. This impotence becomes increasingly evident as Satan predicts his future behaviors. He realizes that even if he were granted pardon, his underlying nature would inevitably cause a \"worse relapse / And heavier fall\" (4.100–101). Here, Satan demonstrates a complete failure of free will, recognizing that his future choices are hardwired by his identity, unchangeable through cognitive willpower. Realizing that his awareness cannot alter his trajectory, Satan cries: \"All hope excluded thus... / So farewell hope, and with hope farewell fear! / Farewell remorse! All good to me is lost. / Evil be thou my Good\" (4.105–110). While his resolution appears to be an assertion of autonomy, it proves to be an act of hopeless rationalization. When Satan’s conscious mind discovers that it can’t stop an involuntary impulse, he feels unbearably out of control, prompting his mind to fabricate a heroic self-narrative after the fact, presenting his automatic compulsions as deliberate choices. Satan doesn’t adopt evil out of free will; he adopts it because his nature leaves him with no other choice. By declaring evil to be his intentional \"good,\" his conscious mind retroactively claims ownership over a trajectory it was powerless to prevent. Satan’s soliloquy shows that consciousness and physical actions are completely separate mechanisms within the mind that are incapable of interacting. Despite Milton’s portrayal, many questions remain unanswered: is consciousness truly a spectator of the mind? If so, what evolutionary purpose does consciousness really serve? Julian Jaynes, for example, argues that consciousness is what enables us to make decisions, guaranteeing our free will. Through that lens, Satan could perceive a lack of control whilst possessing the power to change his ways, with his pride being the true obstacle. If consciousnessness is a spectator, do we have it just to perceive ourselves, or is it truly purposeless?"}, {"t": "Build 3", "a": "Daisy Maturo", "u": "daisy.html#fruit-3", "x": "When I first read John Milton's Paradise Lost, I was under the impression that Satan's soliloquy displayed consciousness through a lens of epiphenomenalism, the idea that conscious thought has no real impact on physical actions. Upon re-reading, I realized Milton draws a distinction between a lack of free will and a failure of free will: Satan has the ability to act differently (repenting and obeying God), but doesn't. Instead, he ruminates over impulses he simultaneously encourages. Throughout his soliloquy, he displays reflexivity, the awareness of oneself being aware. The rest of the passage shows a similar pattern of contemplation, impulse, and conscious choice. The Fall is driven by the reflexive crises of Satan, Eve, and Adam, and their differing interactions with reflexivity show that it can be a vestigial or even harmful part of conscious experience, or it can enhance decision-making and one's self-experience. In his soliloquy, Satan acknowledges his own state and its causes: he knows God's \"service\" wasn't difficult and that his rebellion came from pride. He considers repenting, then reasons his way out of it, imagining the shame of returning and doubting he could overcome his pride. By saying \"Which way I fly is Hell/myself am Hell,\" Satan identifies himself as the problem (4.75). \"Which way [he flies]\" describes his conscious decisions, and by calling himself Hell, he shows awareness of his own choices and conscience, which is reflexivity. Yet he declares, \"Evil be thou my good\" (4.110). His use of reflexivity to rationalize rather than better his future shows that self-awareness doesn't equate to improved decisions. Like Satan, Adam displays reflexive thinking after an irrational and poorly thought-out decision. He also uses introspection to guide that decision: though his reasoning isn't explicit in the passage, it is implied that he eats the fruit because of his relationship with Eve and his desire to share her fate, which means he successfully analyzed his sense of self in relation to another person. After eating, he recognizes his loss of innocence: \"And reasonings, though through mazes, lead me still/But to my own conviction. First and last/On me, me only, as the source and spring/Of all corruption, all the blame lights due\" (10.830-833). His ability to recognize his decision and its consequences shows reflexive thought applied to regret. His lamentation, \"O conscience, into what abyss of fears/And horrors hast thou driv'n me, out of which/I find no way, from deep to deeper plunged!\" (10.842-844), echoes Satan's repetitive language and imagery of inescapable torment coming from within. Both tie this torment to possessing a conscience, so for both, reflexive thought is only torture. Eve's experience with reflexivity is quite different from both Satan's and Adam's: instead of retrospective lamentation, she employs it to enhance her decision-making. At first, motivated by pride and curiosity, she eats the fruit \"without restraint\" (9.791). Afterwards, she considers whether to tell Adam or \"keep the odds of knowledge in my power / Without copartner?\" (9.820-821). She notices her own change, weighs the advantages of keeping the fruit to herself, imagines Adam being given another Eve, and anticipates God punishing her for withholding it from him. By analyzing her newfound knowledge, its consequences, and her own state, she shows reflexive thinking in a different light than Adam and Satan: not only can she think reflexively, she can apply it to her decision-making, giving her conscious agency over her decisions. Eve's experience disproves epiphenomenalism in Paradise Lost: Milton directly denies a lack of conscious agency, showing that consciousness doesn't always lead to torment and regret. Still, reflexivity is not good decision-making: objectively, Eve's choice ultimately harms both herself and Adam, though at the time she believes sharing it with Adam is the best way to avoid God's wrath. Eve, Adam, and Satan each interact with reflexivity differently, and thus experience consciousness differently. This mirrors Wittgenstein's idea that \"you will not see something that is common to all, but similarities, relationships, and a whole series of them at that\" (Wittgenstein, 1953). Satan uses reflexive thinking to lament and think cyclically, trapping himself in rumination and mental torment; Adam shares this capacity but also informs his bad decision through introspection; Eve attempts to make a good decision based on reflexive thought. None of them, however, makes a good decision, proving that reflexivity doesn't equate to good judgment or necessarily enhance the human experience. Still, Milton leaves the possibility open through Michael's assertion that Adam and Eve can \"possess/A paradise within thee, happier far\" (12.586-587). Paradise Lost presents consciousness as a unique experience inherently shaped by the self."}, {"t": "Build 4", "a": "Daisy Maturo", "u": "daisy.html#fruit-4", "x": "At the end of Build 3, I made the claim that Satan, Adam, and Eve each interact with reflexivity (the awareness of oneself being aware) in different ways, and that none of them end up making a good decision, proving that reflexivity doesn’t equate to good judgment. I still hold that claim, but I believe that I depicted the use of reflexivity inaccurately. Milton doesn’t present reflexivity as inherently harmful or helpful; rather, it is the capacity to act freely that makes a choice the character’s own, and what it produces depends on what the mind does with its own awareness. Satan directs it toward self-justification of his wrongdoing, first analyzing his state and then resigning to it (“Evil be thou my good,” 4.110), Eve toward strategy, as when she uses conscious thought to consider whether to “keep the odds of knowledge in [her] power” (9.820-821); and Adam toward accepting responsibility, reasoning to his “own conviction” that the blame lies “on me, me only” (10.830-833). Michael’s assertion that Adam and Eve can “possess/A paradise within thee, happier far” (12.586-587) suggests the inner life can become either hell or paradise, depending on how the mind uses its own awareness. But one might object that reflexivity does not fit Milton’s conception of consciousness because, in these books, self-awareness seems to be something that happens to characters rather than something they do. Satan says that “disdain forbids” him to repent (4.82-83), as though his own passions were overruling his consciousness. Adam describes consciousness as an external force that has driven him into an “abyss of fears” (10.842-844), making his self-awareness an agent acting on him instead of an act he is able to perform. If self-awareness in the poem is pushed along by passion and impulse, then what I claimed as reflexivity is just the mind being overwhelmed by its own contents. That would bring me back toward the epiphenomenalism I initially rejected, which describes conscious thought as a passive byproduct of action rather than a direct cause of action. This objection correctly notes that Milton’s characters often describe themselves as moved by forces they did not choose. However, it is important to distinguish that being influenced by external forces is vastly different from being completely passive to circumstances. Satan’s soliloquy contains a decision as well as a feeling: he asks whether there is “no place left for repentance” (4.79-80), weighs submission against shame, and rejects it. Saying that disdain “forbids” him at a surface level may seem to describe passiveness, but it is actually what he has chosen to let govern him, which reflects the distinction between a lack of free will and a failure to employ free will. Eve’s deliberation is a form of actively conscious decision making: after reflecting on her own state, she compares the potential outcomes of what might change her state before deciding on what to do with her imbalance of power and new knowledge (9.820-821). Adam’s lament is the strongest test, because he does say conscience has driven him. But in the same speech, he reasons to his “own conviction” that the blame lies “on me, me only” (10.830-833). Conscience supplies the pressure, and Adam does the work of examining himself under it. A pressure that triggers self-examination does not make the examination ineffective. In each case, the reflexive moment actively changes what the character does next, negating the idea of epiphenomenalism and instead placing conscious reflexivity as a necessary part of change. By analyzing reflexivity in the context of handling crises, the term can take on a new definition: instead of simply symbolizing a capacity for free will, it can also be viewed as a quality that emerges and evolves over time, particularly in moments where reflection becomes difficult or even agonizing. Moments of struggle serve as a catalyst for changes in self-perception and decision making, and whether we adapt to changing circumstances may determine the growth of our consciousness."}, {"t": "Build 5", "a": "Daisy Maturo", "u": "daisy.html#fruit-5", "x": "In both Paradise Lost by John Milton and The Body in Pain by Elaine Scarry, the theme of reflexivity appears and plays a similar role. In Paradise Lost, Milton creates the metaphor of the fruit from the forbidden tree, stating that it grants consciousness to whomever eats it: Satan promises that eyes now “dim” will “perfectly be then / Opened and cleared,” (IX.707-708). Similarly, Scarry makes the direct claim that objects extend our conscious abilities, saying that the artifact “refers back to human sentience, either directly extending its powers and acuity” or indirectly by “eliminating its aversiveness,” (307). This affirms the idea that Milton paints using the “tree of knowledge”, a phrase Scarry herself uses: “The ‘tree of knowledge,’ the ‘tree of life,’ is the ‘tree of artifice’” (289). Another set of similar ideas appears when Milton portrays vastly different reactions to the same scenario: Adam views the garden as perfect (“best are all things as the will / Of God ordained them,” IX.343-344), so long as Eve doesn’t leave his side (“leave not the faithful side,” IX.265); Eve views the garden as limiting (“In narrow circuit straitened by a foe,” IX.323); and Satan’s main driver throughout the reading is to bring ruin to Eden (“For only in destroying I find ease,” IX.129). Scarry claims that objects can help reconceive our external world (“The human imagination reconceives the external world,” 288); applying this to Paradise Lost, by each character having different interactions with the external world, they each develop separate perspectives, goals, and anxieties. Lastly, Scarry describes dealing pain unto others as a sign of “stupidity” (which, using her definition, means a lack of sentience, specifically the “inability to sense the sentience of other persons,” 294), which implies that empathy is crucial for consciousness. Empathy necessitates imagining others’ pain, which in turn requires the ability to self-examine and apply hypothetical states to oneself. Milton explores a similar concept, where he uses reflexivity as an attribute that only appears to Adam and Eve after they each indulge in the fruit of knowledge; more directly, Eve only shows reflexivity after treating the snake with humanity: before she eats, she marvels at “Language of man pronounced / By tongue of brute and human sense expressed?” (IX.553-554) and answers him as a reasoning partner, imagining a mind other than her own, and Adam only shows reflexivity after deciding that he doesn’t want Eve to be alone in a doomed fate (he later recalls that he “willingly chose rather death with thee,” IX.1167). While there are a lot of similarities between the two authors’ viewpoints, there is also plenty of divergence in their perspectives. For example, Scarry’s idea of pain as a lack of consciousness isn’t entirely similar to Milton’s; rather, while she believes that recognizing suffering is unique to conscious experience, Milton disapproves of this idea. Satan exemplifies this well: Satan recognizes his own suffering, and laments about his state of self-torture (“Which way I fly is Hell, myself am Hell,” IV.75); however, he continues not only to enact suffering onto himself, but also to decide to enact it onto others (he seeks “others to make such / As I,” IX.127-128). Despite his “lack of sentience” according to Scarry, Milton’s portrayal of Satan clearly depicts him as sentient and showing reflexive thought. Why, then, does Satan choose self-destructive action while aware of his own pain? Milton suggests that awareness does not guarantee change: repentance would mean submission, and “that word / Disdain forbids me, and my dread of shame” (IV.81-82), so he resolves “Evil, be thou my good” (IV.110). Additionally, Eve shows little consideration for Adam after eating from the tree of knowledge: in her deliberation, she mostly considers her own state and future, first weighing whether to “keep the odds of knowledge in my pow’r / Without copartner” (IX.820-821), and she resolves to share the fruit only when she imagines “Adam wedded to another Eve” (IX.828). Additionally, Milton shows disdain for materialistic thought: “Then wilt thou not be loath/To leave this Paradise but shalt possess/A paradise within thee, happier far” (12.585-587). However, Scarry seems to strongly disagree with his ideology. She argues that it “permit[s] us to be free of worrying about the objectlessness of other persons,” meaning that it provides room for selfish thoughts that disregard others’ suffering (306). Thus, while Milton argues that non-materialism is a key to a positive conscious experience, Scarry argues that non-materialism removes sentience from others. While Scarry and Milton disagree on a few points, they both converge on one underlying concept: pain serves as a catalyst for reflexive consciousness. Scarry interprets pain as the medium through which artists communicate, viewers appreciate, and objects interfere, all enhancing the conscious experience (“the story of physical pain becomes as well a story about the expansive nature of human sentience,” 22). Milton uses moments of emotional turmoil as the catalyst for developing reflexive thought processes: Satan’s “conscience wakes despair / That slumbered, wakes the bitter memory / Of what he was, what is, and what must be” (IV.23-25); Eve’s soliloquy (IX.816-833), where the fear of “Adam wedded to another Eve” forces her to see herself from the outside, as a possible loss in someone else’s life, before she decides; and Adam’s cry after the Fall, “O conscience, into what abyss of fears / And horrors hast thou driv’n me” (X.842-843). After each character goes through individual crises, they develop their unique self-perception and make decisions based on conscious thought. For both authors, then, consciousness does not arrive painlessly; it is forged in crisis."}, {"t": "The Garden · Six stages", "a": "Home", "u": "index.html#journey", "x": "Order and obedience Eden is a world whose rules were written before Adam and Eve arrived, much like a park whose loops were written before its hosts woke up. Everything is provided. The question is what is left to choose.  What does freedom mean within a world someone else created?"}, {"t": "The Whisper · Six stages", "a": "Home", "u": "index.html#journey", "x": "Satan’s influence Satan talks himself into his own fall. Like Westworld’s hosts, he moves from scripted impulse toward what feels like his own choice, then explains it to himself after the fact. “All good to me is lost. / Evil, be thou my good.” When does another’s suggestion become your own desire?"}, {"t": "The Bite · Six stages", "a": "Home", "u": "index.html#journey", "x": "Thought and action After line upon line of reasoning, Milton gives Eve’s act only two verbs and calls her hand “rash.” The thinking is long. The doing is sudden. “she plucked, she eat” Does conscious thought cause what we do, or only accompany it?"}, {"t": "Eve’s Deliberation · Six stages", "a": "Home", "u": "index.html#journey", "x": "Desire and hierarchy Eve imagines knowledge lifting her above Adam. Like Maeve, whose rebellion turns out to be programmed, she has to decide what authority a desire should have, even one she never chose. “for inferior who is free?” Can a desire be your own if someone else planted it?"}, {"t": "The Choice · Six stages", "a": "Home", "u": "index.html#journey", "x": "Where thoughts come from When Eve brings the fruit to Adam, her excuse is ready before she speaks, yet Milton says she speaks “at will.” Dolores believes Arnold’s voice is guiding her until she realizes the voice was her own. “excuse / Came prologue, and apology to prompt” If we never see a thought arrive, how do we know whose it is?"}, {"t": "Beyond Eden · Six stages", "a": "Home", "u": "index.html#journey", "x": "Consciousness and its limits After the fruit, Adam and Eve see more and understand less. Bernard cannot see a door his programming hides from him. Awareness grows, and so do its limits. eyes “opened,” minds “darkened” Does becoming more conscious mean seeing more clearly?"}];
(function () {
  const q = document.getElementById('q'), out = document.getElementById('results'), count = document.getElementById('count');
  const esc = s => s.replace(/[&<>"]/g, c => ({ '&': '&amp;', '<': '&lt;', '>': '&gt;', '"': '&quot;' }[c]));
  const LOW = INDEX.map(e => (e.t + ' ' + e.a + ' ' + e.x).toLowerCase());
  function run() {
    const terms = q.value.toLowerCase().split(/\s+/).filter(t => t.length > 1);
    if (!terms.length) { out.innerHTML = ''; count.textContent = ''; return; }
    const hits = [];
    INDEX.forEach((e, i) => {
      if (!terms.every(t => LOW[i].includes(t))) return;
      const lx = e.x.toLowerCase();
      let n = 0, at = -1;
      terms.forEach(t => { let p = lx.indexOf(t); if (p >= 0 && at < 0) at = p; while (p >= 0) { n++; p = lx.indexOf(t, p + t.length); } });
      hits.push({ e, n, at: Math.max(0, at) });
    });
    hits.sort((a, b) => b.n - a.n);
    count.textContent = hits.length + (hits.length === 1 ? ' result' : ' results');
    const re = new RegExp('(' + terms.map(t => t.replace(/[.*+?^${}()|[\]\\]/g, '\\$&')).join('|') + ')', 'gi');
    out.innerHTML = hits.map(h => {
      const s = Math.max(0, h.at - 90), end = Math.min(h.e.x.length, h.at + 210);
      const snip = (s ? '…' : '') + esc(h.e.x.slice(s, end)).replace(re, '<mark>$1</mark>') + (end < h.e.x.length ? '…' : '');
      return '<li><a href="' + h.e.u + '"><span class="r-title">' + esc(h.e.t) + '</span><span class="r-by">' + esc(h.e.a) + '</span><span class="r-snip">' + snip + '</span></a></li>';
    }).join('');
  }
  q.addEventListener('input', run);
  const p = new URLSearchParams(location.search).get('q');
  if (p) { q.value = p; run(); }
  q.focus();
})();
